    ## Summary
    
    - add the `arty_tokio` compatibility crate with the approved minimal
    public API
    - install one Tokio runtime context on every fixed Arty worker with
    transactional startup and cleanup
    - accept caller-owned multi-thread Tokio runtime handles by value or by
    reference
    - reject current-thread runtimes and preserve native Tokio task, panic,
    timer, blocking, and I/O behavior
    - document lifecycle limits and provide runnable external-runtime and
    Tokio-library examples
    
    ## Example
    
    ```rust
    use arty::task::Context;
    
    async fn call_tokio_based_library() -> u64 {
        tokio::time::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(10)).await;
        42
    }
    
    #[arty::main]
    async fn main(cx: Context) -> Result<(), ohno::AppError> {
        let tokio = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_multi_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()?;
    
        arty_tokio::init(&cx, tokio.handle()).await?;
        assert_eq!(call_tokio_based_library().await, 42);
        Ok(())
    }
    ```
    
    The application owns the Tokio runtime and must keep it alive while Arty
    tasks use Tokio.
    
