    feat!: accept caller-selected memory providers (#895)
    
    [Copilot speaking]
    
    ## Purpose and scope
    
    Applications need to choose the memory provider that supplies byte
    storage for HTTP bodies and serialized cache entries. Requiring
    `GlobalPool` in public constructors prevents that choice. This PR
    accepts `OpaqueMemory`, an owned wrapper around a caller-selected
    `MemoryShared` provider.
    
    Callers select the provider when they construct a builder, client, or
    serialization boundary. The resulting objects retain it for later
    operations. This is construction-time selection, not a mechanism for
    replacing the provider inside an existing client.
    
    ```text
    Caller-selected provider
              |
         OpaqueMemory
              |
              +--> HTTP body builder --> body byte storage
              |
              +--> HTTP client -------> transport body operations
              |                         (including WinHTTP reads and writes)
              |
              +--> Cache serialization --> encoded keys and values
    ```
    
    ## Public API changes
    
    These are source-breaking changes. Wrap an existing global pool with
    `OpaqueMemory::new(pool)` to retain that choice, or supply another
    provider. Fake constructors continue to select `GlobalPool`.
    
    | API | Required caller input |
    | --- | --- |
    | `HttpBodyBuilder::new` | `OpaqueMemory` and `&Clock` |
    | `HttpBodyBuilder::with_custom_memory` | Removed. Use
    `HttpBodyBuilder::new(OpaqueMemory::new(provider), clock)` |
    | `fetch::custom::CustomDeps` | `memory: OpaqueMemory` replaces
    `global_pool` |
    | `fetch::tokio::TokioDeps` | `memory: OpaqueMemory` replaces
    `global_pool` |
    | `WinHttpDeps::builder` | `Clock`, `OpaqueMemory`, and `Sink` |
    | `CacheBuilder::serialize` and `FallbackBuilder::serialize` | An
    explicit `OpaqueMemory` argument |
    
    `HttpBodyBuilder::new` is the single constructor for caller-selected
    memory. The Hyper transport continues to accept a custom body builder.
    Other APIs that already accept a suitable provider retain their existing
    signatures.
    
    ## Examples
    
    A caller can pass its provider to a body builder once, then reuse the
    builder for multiple bodies. The function accepts any compatible
    provider, rather than choosing a pool internally:
    
    ```rust
    use bytesbuf::mem::{MemoryShared, OpaqueMemory};
    use http_extensions::{HttpBody, HttpBodyBuilder};
    use tick::Clock;
    
    fn build_bodies(provider: impl MemoryShared, clock: &Clock) -> (HttpBody, HttpBody) {
        let builder = HttpBodyBuilder::new(OpaqueMemory::new(provider), clock);
        let first = builder.text("first body");
        let second = builder.slice(b"second body");
        (first, second)
    }
    ```
    
    To keep using the global pool, pass `GlobalPool::new()` as that
    function's provider. A custom provider follows the same path. For
    repeated direct reservations, use `client.reserve(size)` or acquire
    `client.memory()` once and retain its result outside the loop.
    
    Cache serialization uses the supplied provider for both encoded keys and
    encoded values. This example uses an in-memory fallback that stores
    serialized bytes. It requires cachet's `serialize` and `memory`
    features:
    
    ```rust
    use bytesbuf::BytesView;
    use bytesbuf::mem::{MemoryShared, OpaqueMemory};
    use cachet::Cache;
    use tick::Clock;
    
    fn configure_cache(clock: Clock, provider: impl MemoryShared) {
        let fallback = Cache::builder::<BytesView, BytesView>(clock.clone()).memory();
        let cache = Cache::builder::<String, String>(clock)
            .memory()
            .serialize(OpaqueMemory::new(provider))
            .fallback(fallback)
            .build();
    
        // Retain `cache` for subsequent cache operations.
        let _ = cache;
    }
    ```
    
    The provider controls serialization byte storage, not every allocation
    performed by the cache or its dependencies. The serialized format
    remains unchanged.
    
    ## Provider lifetime and efficiency
    
    Body construction, cache encoding, and client reservations borrow stored
    providers instead of repeatedly acquiring owners. WinHTTP borrows the
    transport's body builder and the upload provider during a request. Its
    response reader retains an owned provider because the response can
    outlive the transport call.
    
    Explicit provider cloning remains meaningful: each provider decides
    whether its clones share state. Relocation, which notifies an object on
    its destination thread, still reaches the provider. Existing buffers
    retain their original storage, and provider selection does not change
    body lengths, response limits, or timeouts.
    
    [Benchmark results: baseline versus latest
    PR](https://github.com/microsoft/oxidizer/pull/895#issuecomment-6462393213)
    compare only the pre-PR baseline with the latest runtime behavior in
    this PR. Both have zero measured heap allocations for sustained client
    reservations. Median reservation times are 5.5% and 7.5% lower in the PR
    for the tested sizes. The report retains all slower cases and explains
    shared-host variation, which limits conclusions about timing
    differences.
    
    Release-version increments and dependency requirement changes are
    separate in [#897](https://github.com/microsoft/oxidizer/pull/897),
    stacked above this code change. This PR does not increment package
    versions.
    
