    ## Summary
    
    Soft-stabilizes the `tick` public API. This PR uses one deliberate
    breaking-change window to simplify names, remove accidental commitments,
    and establish the contracts that future releases will preserve.
    
    The goal is to finish the API polish now so later work can remain
    additive and compatible.
    
    - `Timer` replaces `PeriodicTimer` and provides `async fn next(&mut
    self) -> TimerTick` without exposing `futures_core::Stream`.
    - `InactiveClock` requires an explicit timer-storage mode through
    `new_isolated()` or `new_shared()`.
    - `Timeout` has one generic parameter and always uses `Delay` as its
    deadline.
    - `Error` exposes stable classification and backtrace accessors.
    - Timer wake handling is allocation-free and does not invoke or drop
    wakers while internal locks are held.
    - `ClockControl` keeps its existing fluent API.
    - `Clock::system_time_as` and `SimpleClock::system_time_as` remain
    infallible for practical production use. OS clock values are normalized,
    and all supported `tick::fmt` types cover the current production range.
    
    ```text
    Timer::next
      wait for the existing deadline
      return TimerTick { observed_at }
      preserve the deadline if the next future is cancelled
    
    futures application
      adapt Timer with futures::stream::unfold
      keep futures outside tick's public dependency surface
    ```
    
    ## Public API changes
    
    | Surface | Stabilized contract | Migration |
    |---|---|---|
    | `Timer` | Renames `PeriodicTimer`, removes the public `Stream`
    implementation, and adds inherent `next() -> TimerTick` | Rename the
    type. Replace stream combinators with `next().await` loops, or use the
    documented downstream `unfold` adapter. |
    | `TimerTick` | A non-`Clone`, non-`Copy` result with `observed_at() ->
    Instant` | Read the clock-domain instant from the returned tick. |
    | `Timeout` | Changes `Timeout<F, D = Delay>` to `Timeout<F>` | Remove
    explicit deadline generic arguments. Use `FutureExt::timeout`. |
    | `Stopwatch` | Removes conversions to `Duration` and `Instant` | Use
    `Stopwatch::elapsed()`. |
    | `Error` | Adds `is_timeout()`, `is_out_of_range()`, and `backtrace()
    -> &Backtrace`; timeout errors retain a `TimedOut` source | Use the
    classification methods instead of matching messages. Use
    `backtrace().status()` to detect disabled capture. |
    | `Result` | Removes `tick::Result<T>` | Use `Result<T, tick::Error>` or
    an application error type. |
    | `InactiveClock` | Removes `Default`; adds explicit `new_isolated()`
    beside `new_shared()` | Choose the timer-storage model at construction.
    |
    | `ClockControl` | Keeps the established fluent constructors and
    configuration methods | No migration. |
    | Time conversion | Keeps `system_time_as` infallible | Use direct
    `TryFrom<SystemTime>` only when conversion failure is expected by the
    application. |
    
    No public API changes are intended in `fetch`, `fetch_hyper`,
    `http_extensions`, `seatbelt`, or `seatbelt_http`.
    
    ## Stabilization boundary
    
    After this PR, new `tick` capabilities must normally be additive. Public
    renames, removals, generic-shape changes, and dependency traits in the
    public surface require a new explicit breaking-change decision.
    
    The following contracts are intentional:
    
    - `Timer` uses inherent async iteration and does not implement
    `futures_core::Stream`.
    - `ClockControl` has no builder.
    - `InactiveClock` construction is explicit.
    - `system_time_as` is infallible for supported production formats.
    - Error classification uses methods, not public enum variants or message
    parsing.
    
    ## Performance
    
    Metabench allocation probes report zero allocations for idle,
    five-pending, and five-ready timer advancement. The five-ready case
    improved from one 456-byte allocation to zero.
    
    Sequential Criterion comparison on the same host measured:
    
    | Scenario | Before | After | Change |
    |---|---:|---:|---:|
    | Five ready timers | 195.29 ns | 160.11 ns | -18.0% |
    | Composite clock workload | 925.17 ns | 899.51 ns | -2.8% |
    | Idle | 28.94 ns | 34.08 ns | +5.14 ns |
    | Five pending timers | 36.10 ns | 39.56 ns | +3.46 ns |
    
