    [Copilot speaking]
    
    I/O providers need a clear contract for worker initialization and
    repeated driver creation. The `IoContext` documentation defines the
    required sequence: clone the registered context, relocate it to the
    async worker, then create its driver.
    
    The runtime calls `driver()` at most once per concrete context-type
    registration and async worker, not once per context clone.
    Implementations can reject duplicate driver creation and immediately
    reject application-facing operations on contexts without successful
    initialization. Driver construction errors remain fatal, with no retry
    requirement.
    
    Clones of initialized contexts remain usable without another driver
    call. The initial setup requirement does not change the general
    `ThreadAware` movement contract: later relocation remains optional and
    affects performance only.
    
    The panic tests cover propagation and cleanup of existing drivers
    without retrying provider initialization. Production runtime behavior is
    unchanged.

