    perf(seismograph_runtime): key the live task registry by task ID (#879)
    
    ## Summary
    
    `seismograph_runtime` keeps each runtime's live tasks in a
    `Mutex<Vec<Arc<TaskControl>>>`. Retiring a task runs `Vec::retain` over
    the whole list while holding that lock. Every task completion therefore
    costs O(live tasks), and all workers serialize on one lock.
    
    This change stores live tasks in a `HashMap` keyed by task ID, so
    registration and retirement are O(1). Snapshots (a cold path) sort tasks
    by ID, so their order stays deterministic.
    
    ## Why
    
    We found this while load-testing an HTTP.sys server built on the
    Oxidizer runtime, on a 44-core Azure HBv4 VM with HTTP/2 at 8,192
    requests in flight (256 connections × 32 streams). One task per request
    means thousands of live tasks.
    
    | Build | Throughput | p99 latency |
    |---|---:|---:|
    | `seismograph_runtime` 0.2.0 | ~20k req/s | ~930 ms |
    | Same build, keyed map (this change) | ~200k req/s | ~111 ms |
    
    At around 512 requests in flight the cost is invisible. It grows with
    the number of live tasks.
    
    ## Validation
    
    - `cargo test -p seismograph_runtime --all-features`: all tests pass,
    including a new test that retires tasks out of order and checks the
    remaining live set, counters and snapshot order.
    - `cargo clippy -p seismograph_runtime --all-targets --all-features`: no
    new warnings.
    
