    test: scope tracing initialization to direct dependencies (#881)
    
    [Copilot speaking]
    
    Tests need a consistent tracing initialization rule so coverage and
    mutation testing exercise tracing fields and formatting without imposing
    obligations on unrelated consumers.
    
    Every Cargo unit-test and integration-test executable in a package with
    a direct normal or dev-dependency on `tracing` initializes tracing
    before tests run. The rule applies across declared features and
    platforms. Build-only dependencies and procedural-macro host
    dependencies do not qualify a consumer. An individual target that
    explicitly exercises or captures tracing through a dependency also
    initializes tracing.
    
    Tracing caches interest within each process. Each qualifying integration
    target owns its initializer because a dependency's unit-test initializer
    does not run in the consumer executable. A dependency's internal tracing
    belongs in that dependency's tests. Transitive presence alone creates no
    consumer testing obligation.
    
    The repository guide, agent guidance, and helper documentation define
    this boundary and the Cargo target audit procedure. Covered targets
    initialize tracing, while unrelated consumer targets omit initialization
    and unnecessary constructor/helper dependencies. Other uses of test
    helpers remain available.
    
    Tests that deliberately disable tracing, require no subscriber, or own a
    custom global subscriber use separate Cargo targets with a documented
    purpose. The existing no-subscriber target remains isolated.
