    perf(performables): skip redundant handle clone on warm relocation (#876)
    
    ## Summary
    
    `performables::arc::relocate_affinity` cloned `state.current` before
    acquiring the storage lock. On a warm relocation, the destination
    partition already exists and the function discards that clone in favor
    of the stored handle. Deferring the clone removes an unnecessary atomic
    reference-count increment and decrement from this path.
    
    The old eager clone also kept the replaced allocation alive until after
    the write guard was released. Removing the clone must preserve that
    guarantee: this change explicitly releases the storage write guard
    before replacing the warm-path handle. The regression test verifies that
    the replaced value's destructor runs outside the lock. This preserves
    existing behavior rather than claiming a pre-existing destructor
    deadlock.
    
    This change comes from the ThreadAware existing-implementation audit,
    AB#8082605.
    
    ## Changes
    
    - Defer the current-handle clone until after the warm-hit return.
    - Release the write guard before assigning the warm-hit handle.
    - Retain `performables_arc_relocate`, with warm same-partition and
    round-trip scenarios for `PerThread` and `PerNuma`.
    - Retain the destructor/lock regression test.
    - Merge current `main`, keeping both this benchmark registration and
    upstream's ready/watch benchmark registrations.
    
    ## Benchmark
    
    `cargo bench -p performables --bench performables_arc_relocate`
    
    | Scenario | Before | After | Change |
    |---|---:|---:|---:|
    | per_thread/same | 37.380 ns | 33.571 ns | -10.2% |
    | per_thread/roundtrip | 82.890 ns | 72.775 ns | -12.1% |
    | per_numa/same | 34.977 ns | 32.667 ns | -7.5% |
    | per_numa/roundtrip | 70.587 ns | 66.077 ns | -6.6% |
    
    These are historical measurements from the original Criterion-only
    harness, before the main merge and the metabench conversion. They are
    not timings from the current harness, which adds a non-inlined workload
    boundary. Criterion used 50 samples, a 1-second warm-up, and a 3-second
    measurement period; all four comparisons reported p below 0.05. These
    are single-threaded, uncontended relocation-call microbenchmarks, not
    cross-core or NUMA-throughput measurements. All four benchmark smoke
    scenarios also pass after the merge.
    
