    perf(arty): reduce scheduler contention (#855)
    
    ## Summary
    
    This change makes each `arty_executor::Executor` truly single-threaded:
    its task ownership, ready queues, wake processing, and cycle state are
    owned and mutated only by the executor thread.
    
    The scheduler hot path no longer relies on mutex-protected shared state
    or hash-map lookups to find and retain tasks. Tasks use intrusive
    ownership and worker-local scheduling state instead, keeping local
    spawn, poll, wake, and completion work direct and allocation-conscious.
    
    Cross-thread wakes are delivered through the remote wake path and
    drained in batches by the owner thread. Notifications use a coalescing
    latch, so bursts of remote wakes request worker attention without
    requiring one operating-system wake per task wake.
    
    The branch also retains task ownership across poll unwinding, preventing
    a panicking future from releasing the executor's final task reference
    before cleanup completes. Enrichment transfer remains mandatory; the
    optional transfer fast path is deferred to a separate PR.
    
    ## Performance
    
    These are representative Criterion results from an internal run on
    Windows/x86-64 with Rust 1.96.1 and `-C target-cpu=x86-64-v3`; exact
    timings vary by machine.
    
