    ## Performance evidence
    
    **These timings are historical measurements before the later base
    integrations and review follow-ups, not measurements of the current PR
    head.** They came from a real Azure Standard_HB176rs_v4 with 176
    available physical cores, SMT off, Rust 1.95, ordinary
    release/x86-64-v3, and telemetry compiled in but disabled. The requested
    172-worker configuration was included.
    
    Critical watch cancellation workload, 256 retained pending waits per
    worker; microseconds per round, with one operation per worker per round.
    Values average two matched Criterion point estimates, not pooled
    confidence intervals:
    
    | Workers | Performables | Tokio | std-mutex fixture |
    | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
    | 172 | 43.31 | 109.68 | 3109.51 |
    | 176 | 43.73 | 111.19 | 3214.78 |
    
    At 176 workers, this is 60.7% less time than Tokio and 98.6% less than
    the fixture. **std has no watch primitive**: the control is a simple
    mutex-backed boolean broadcast fixture with linear cancellation, not a
    native std watch. These results do not imply linear core scaling.
    
    Actual uncontended primitive comparisons on one pinned core,
    nanoseconds:
    
    | Operation | Performables | std | Tokio |
    | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
    | Sync Mutex lock/unlock | 4.63 | 4.36 | 16.92 |
    | Sync RwLock read | 5.21 | 4.93 | 16.67 |
    | Sync RwLock write | 5.43 | 4.56 | 16.89 |
    | Ready bounded blocking send + receive | 14.97 | 19.30 | 52.10 |
    
