    agent, cloud agent, and reviewer receives the same reviewed instructions
    without a network fetch or personal installation. The vendored copy is
    pinned to upstream v2.1.1 and includes GitHub provenance metadata, its
    reference files, and the MIT license.
    
    The Contributors discussion also identified ambiguity in the phrase
    "prefer integration tests." The intended policy is to test actual
    behavior through public APIs and controlled test utilities, generally
    without real I/O. Unit tests remain appropriate for implementation paths
    that public APIs cannot reach.
    
    This distinction matters for agentic changes. Tests based on public
    behavior remain stable through refactoring and performance work. They
    also make intentional contract changes visible. Tests coupled to
    internal APIs are easier for an agent to rewrite with the
    implementation, which can hide behavior changes.
    
    The discussion favored one definitive developer-agent guide for writing
    tests instead of overlapping instructions. Review agents remain stricter
    because review comments are advisory and use the existing
    `.github/skills/code-review/test-review.md` policy.
    
    ## Summary
    
    Add concise repository-wide agent instructions that:
    
    - Use a vendored, pinned v2.1.1 `simple-english` project skill for crate
    documentation and other human-facing documentation.
    - Invoke a repository-owned `writing-tests` skill when tests change.
    - Test public behavior through public APIs and test utilities.
    - Expose test-only public APIs behind a non-default `test-util` feature
    when integration tests need controlled dependencies or state.
    - Keep test-only APIs out of the default public surface.
    - Keep tests deterministic, fast, in-memory, and independent of real I/O
    where practical.
    - Use unit tests for implementation details that public APIs cannot
    exercise.
    - Preserve behavior tests during refactoring and disclose intentional
    contract changes in the pull request.
    - Recommend `fakeable`, `future_assert`, `tick`, and `insta` for common
    test needs.
    
    The `writing-tests` skill is for developer agents. The existing
    test-review guide remains the stricter source for review agents.
    
    ## Simple English demonstration
    
    Apply the new documentation policy to the `recoverable` crate in the
    same PR. The update rewrites crate rustdoc, recovery recipes, I/O
    classification guidance, and example documentation in Simple English. It
    preserves the APIs and technical facts, then regenerates
    `crates/recoverable/README.md` from the crate documentation.
    
    This gives reviewers a concrete before-and-after example of the policy
    instead of introducing the instruction without testing its effect on
    real project documentation.
    
    ## Evidence
    
    The `recoverable` documentation passed:
    
    - `just anvil-fmt --fix`
    - `just anvil-readme --fix`
    - `just anvil-doc-test` with 20 of 20 doctests passing both with and
    without all features
    - `just anvil-doc-build` with rustdoc warnings denied
    - `just anvil-spellcheck`
    - `git diff --check`
    
    The `simple-english` skill was installed from the upstream v2.1.1 tag
    with `gh skill install --pin v2.1.1`. Its metadata records the upstream
    repository, tag, and tree SHA.
    
    ## Merge Danger
    
    **Door:** Two-way
    
    A revert removes the policies, both project skills, and the
    documentation rewrite.
    
    **Blast Radius:** Repository
    
    Incorrect guidance can affect future documentation and test changes. The
    demonstration changes documentation only and does not change runtime
    behavior or public APIs.
    
