    feat(seismograph)!: replace TCP monitoring with native IPC (#847)
    
    Local TCP endpoints and bearer tokens make OS-level access control and
    cross-user monitor discovery awkward. Use native local IPC so ordinary
    users can observe their own processes without administrator setup, while
    root or elevated administrators can discover and connect to other users'
    monitors.
    
    ## Approach
    
    - Use Unix-domain sockets and Windows named pipes, enforcing ownership
    and peer identity through OS credentials and permissions rather than
    application secrets.
    - Initialize private per-user registries automatically: XDG runtime
    storage with a secure temporary fallback on Unix, and local
    application-data storage on Windows. Discovery aggregates accessible
    registries instead of requiring a privileged shared application
    directory.
    - Thread a concrete `MonitorIo` capability through the protocol, server,
    and CLI. The public `test-util` feature provides isolated mock
    identities, clocks, filesystem operations, partial transfers, deadlines,
    and cancellation without exposing a backend trait. Feature-gated
    server-connection views and shared read/close observations support
    lifecycle assertions.
    - Bound automatic Hello, cache-policy, and server-version responses to
    64 KiB before payload allocation. A connection-wide absolute 10-second
    deadline survives partial reads, peeks, and writes; failed probes become
    endpoint warnings and scanning continues. Explicit snapshot capture
    retains large frames and unlimited capture reads.
    - Keep pending Windows `OVERLAPPED` storage raw-owned across moves
    without pointee reborrows, preserving cancellation/draining before
    destruction. Project variable-length ACE SIDs from the original native
    pointer, and guard known-folder allocations before inspecting failure
    results.
    - Update monitor discovery and presentation, document feature-gated
    APIs, and add a runnable `performables` workload with real mutex/Arc
    telemetry plus a bounded native integration test. A separate native
    transport test also runs without optional test utilities.
    
    ## Compatibility
    
    The monitor control protocol advances to version 8. Upgrade monitored
    applications and the CLI together; TCP endpoints and bearer-token
    handshakes are removed. The native snapshot format is unchanged. Normal
    builder startup requires no provisioning step.
    
    Windows registries must use direct local-disk paths. Network locations,
    substituted drives, and reparse-point ancestors are rejected. Directory
    traversal, creation, and enumeration use local handles so elevated
    discovery does not follow profile-controlled redirections or reopen an
    unchecked pathname. Named-pipe clients permit identification, not
    privilege impersonation.
    
    Unix socket creation uses atomic close-on-exec on Linux and the
    supported BSD targets. Platforms without that facility, including Apple
    platforms, use an explicit fallback; applications using raw fork/exec
    must serialize spawning with connection creation.
    
    ## Validation
    
    - Required CI passed on `c482e8ae`. The integration-test feature-gating
    follow-up in `d7ba76e3` needs fresh CI before merge.
    - The `monitor_io` integration target compiles and runs independently on
    Windows and Linux with no features, `monitor`, and `test-util`,
    selecting exactly 0, 3, and 4 tests respectively. Only the
    helper-dependent shutdown test requires Seismograph's `test-util`;
    startup, capture, and isolation tests remain available with `monitor`
    alone. Monitor-only all-target Clippy passes on both hosts.
    - Generated Anvil formatting, workspace Clippy, README regeneration, and
    spelling passed. Before this test-only gate adjustment, affected
    protocol/server tests passed on Windows (283) and Ubuntu WSL (286);
    affected Windows doctests passed with and without default features, and
    the build passed.
    - The most recent **100% measured-line coverage gates** passed locally
    for the protocol and monitor on both Windows and Linux, combining
    separate `--all-features` and `--no-default-features` runs. The Windows
    configurations ran 283 and 239 passing tests; Linux ran 286 and 242.
    Native measurement exclusions are described below.
    - The portable protocol and public mocked-I/O suites passed isolated
    Miri, including the shared read/close observation regression. Raw
    allocation move/access and exactly-once destruction regressions have
    also passed with Tree Borrows.
    - Shutdown regressions synchronize with actual partial-header
    consumption. The native test additionally observes a pending
    kernel/readiness read; the mock observes its second read and explicit
    `IoOperation::Close` without advancing the clock. Both retain
    server-side handles and require explicit shutdown, preventing
    queued-endpoint cleanup or normal handshake expiry from passing. These
    tests passed 20 repeated Windows runs and 10 Linux runs; removing
    shutdown fails the native close assertion immediately.
    - Focused follow-up mutation runs caught the shutdown and
    empty-observation mutations, all four read/close-counter result
    mutations, and five automatically included field mutations with the
    complete monitor suite. The nonconstructible default connection result
    is type-unviable, not counted as caught. No new mutation exclusions were
    added.
    - Earlier automatic-probe regressions reject oversized advertisements
    after the 20-byte header, cover each discovery handshake stage, enforce
    a fixed deadline despite partial progress, continue to healthy monitors,
    and preserve explicit snapshots larger than 64 KiB. The focused
    client/probe scope accounted for 75 candidates: 73 caught and two
    type-unviable; five initially hanging variants were caught after
    bounding the fixtures. Full mutation coverage remains a fresh-CI
    obligation.
    - Native deadline tests fill finite buffers with repeated fixed-size
    chunks, assert transfer progress and returned timeout status, and use
    only generous hang watchdogs. Backlog tests assert requested-wait bounds
    rather than sub-second wall time.
    - Root/owner/other-UID authorization passed for both XDG and temporary
    registries. Native tests retain restricted-token access checks,
    local-only traversal, intermediate junction rejection, handle-based
    enumeration, descriptor validation, pending-read cancellation, and clone
    closure. Real multi-account Windows administration and macOS/BSD
    execution have not been exercised locally.
    - Exercised the interactive workload's readiness, live pipe connection,
    Enter-to-stop behavior, and descriptor cleanup. Generated README files
    and feature-tagged API documentation were regenerated during
    implementation.
    
    Linux LCOV was exported from the actual tested executables because
    cargo-llvm-cov incorrectly discovers kache's JSON metadata as object
    files. The compiler cache remained enabled.
    
    ## Native measurement exclusions
    
    Specific native IPC, OS credential/security queries, profile
    enumeration, completion/cancellation, and cleanup helpers have
    function-scoped coverage and mutation exclusions with inline reasons.
    These cover kernel failures, completion races, live profile state, or
    cleanup diagnostics that cannot be reliably forced without changing
    process privileges or shared host state. Native integration tests still
    execute these helpers.
    
    Windows ACL/grant policy and Unix root/sticky-directory and
    discovery-error policy remain measured and mutation-tested independently
    of OS extraction. Portable parsing, identity validation, size bounds,
    mock transfers, and ownership policy are not excluded.
    
    Exact mutation rules document disjoint native flags where XOR and OR are
    equivalent, the Linux pathname-socket terminator case, and the fd-zero
    boundary requiring process stdin to be closed. Narrow item exclusions
    also document native deallocation, trivial native pointer access,
    unbuffered flush equivalence, scheduler-only mock handoff, and
    definitions compiled out on supported targets. Windows mutation
    configuration excludes only the Unix monitor sources compiled out on
    Windows.
    
    Coverage targets are unchanged. Required CI, including the complete
    mutation tier, must pass against the latest head before this PR is
    ready.
    
