    fix(arty): harden I/O driver hosting (#857)
    
    ## Summary
    
    Follow-up to #840 addressing the still-applicable findings from the
    final review report.
    
    - use a simple fatal registration model: construct and register each
    shared context once, then initialize each host's local driver lazily
    before returning from that host
    - reject same-thread, same-type reentrant initialization across shared
    hosts without adding transactional rollback machinery
    - bind `DriverHost` construction to an explicit current-worker `Thread`
    and accept move-only context options
    - complete lifecycle telemetry and add public coverage for affinity,
    waking, telemetry schemas/order, and Drop behavior
    
    `arty_io_host` remains unstable Arty implementation infrastructure and
    is not positioned for direct application or library use.
    
    ## API and behavior changes
    
    - `IoRuntime::driver_host(self, thread: Thread, owner_waker: Waker) ->
    Result<DriverHost, DriverError>` replaces relocation-derived optional
    thread state.
    - `IoRuntime` no longer implements `ThreadAware`; the supplied worker
    coordinate is validated and used to relocate the sink during host
    construction.
    - `DriverHost::context` consumes `ContextOptions` and no longer requires
    `Clone`.
    - Context construction publishes the shared registration before
    worker-local driver initialization. A driver initialization failure is
    runtime-fatal; another worker may already hold the context, and
    successful drivers are shut down when their hosts are dropped during
    runtime shutdown.
    - A small per-thread initialization guard returns `DriverError` instead
    of recursively initializing or waiting on the same context type through
    another host.
    - Successful shutdown emits `arty.rt.io.driver.stopped`; failure events
    retain the maintainer-selected `error.message` attribute.
    
    ## Review finding dispositions
    
    | # | Disposition | Detail |
    |---|---|---|
    | 1 | Resolved by contract decision | The selected contract treats
    driver initialization failure as fatal rather than transactional.
    Contexts are registered after construction; no rollback/unpublication is
    attempted, and existing drivers remain owned until runtime shutdown
    drops their hosts. Core documentation and tests now state this behavior
    explicitly. |
    | 2 | Fixed | Provider construction/cloning runs outside the global
    registry mutex. Same-thread retrieval of the same type through another
    host is rejected with `DriverError`; concurrent retrieval on other
    threads remains coordinated by `OnceLock`. |
    | 3 | Fixed | Host construction takes an explicit `Thread`, validates
    the current OS thread, returns `DriverError` on mismatch, and no longer
    stores optional relocation state. |
    | 4 | Fixed | `DriverHost::context` takes options by value and removes
    the `Clone` bound; a move-only options integration test covers the
    contract. |
    | 5 | Already fixed in merged #840 | The merged quick start used one
    runtime/worker owner rather than rebuilding owners per worker. It is
    updated here only for the explicit-thread host API. |
    | 6 | Superseded by maintainer direction | Retained `error.message` as
    the idiomatic driver failure attribute and updated telemetry assertions
    to reject `exception.message`. |
    | 7 | Fixed | Added `arty.rt.io.driver.stopped` with matching context
    and role dimensions. |
    | 8 | Fixed | Added justified `#[inline]` to the exported per-cycle hot
    path under `docs/performance.md` rule 1. |
