    perf(tick)!: make timer drivers deadline-driven (#835)
    
    ## Summary
    
    - replace `Clock::new_tokio()`'s fixed 10 ms advancement loop with waits
    for the exact next timer deadline
    - notify every live driver when the first timer or a newly earliest
    timer is registered
    - park indefinitely when no timers exist and wake drivers when the final
    clock owner is dropped
    - require runtime integrations to provide the driver `Waker` through
    `InactiveClock::activate(waker)`
    - isolate Tokio-specific implementation and lifecycle tests in
    `tick::tokio`
    - move publicly observable Tick behavior into integration tests
    
    This adapts the timer-wakeup design from martintmk/oxidizer commit
    [`d190a9fe`](https://github.com/martintmk/oxidizer/commit/d190a9fe78d725cb2772348f273288354c636c90).
    
    ## Breaking change
    
    `InactiveClock::activate()` now requires a `Waker`. Runtime integrations
    use this waker to interrupt an existing wait when the first timer or a
    newly earlier timer is registered. The runtime integration examples use
    a real thread-unparking waker; a no-op waker is not suitable for a
    runtime that parks.
    
    ## Idle behavior evidence
    
    A deterministic paused-Tokio unit test advances virtual time across 100
    ms (ten former polling intervals) and proves that a no-deadline driver
    wait remains pending until notification.
    
    I also repeated a Windows release probe on base `3682cc26` and this
    branch. The probe used a current-thread Tokio runtime, constructed four
    `Clock::new_tokio()` instances, then slept for 10 seconds:
    
    ```rust
    #[tokio::main(flavor = "current_thread")]
    async fn main() {
        let _clocks: [_; 4] = std::array::from_fn(|_| tick::Clock::new_tokio());
        tokio::time::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_secs(10)).await;
    }
    ```
    
    Each revision was built with:
    
    ```powershell
    cargo build -p tick --example tokio_idle_review_probe --features tokio --release
    ```
    
    Each executable was run five times with PowerShell `Start-Process`; CPU
    time was read from `Process.TotalProcessorTime.TotalMilliseconds` after
    exit.
    
    | Revision | Five CPU-time samples (ms) | Observed range |
    |---|---|---|
    | Base fixed 10 ms loop | 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 | below the 15.625 ms accounting
    quantum |
    | Deadline-driven loop | 0, 15.625, 0, 15.625, 15.625 | below or equal
    to one accounting quantum |
    
    This probe is too coarse to establish magnitude or statistical
    significance, and it does not demonstrate a directional CPU difference.
    The deterministic notification-only test establishes the behavioral
    guarantee; a higher-resolution ETW/per-thread wakeup experiment is still
    needed for a quantitative Windows claim.
    
