    refactor(arty): harden task and executor lifecycle
    
    ## Summary
    
    This PR removes unsafe caller lifecycle contracts from `arty_executor`,
    defines cloned-waker behavior, and makes the executor the single owner
    of task completion, cancellation, panic transport, result delivery, and
    lifecycle telemetry.
    
    **Performance improved alongside the lifecycle simplification.** At the
    exact measured head, Arty contention uses 8.4% fewer instructions with
    one worker and 10.9% fewer with eight. Pooled remote result channels
    reduce each Arty case from 130 allocations / 5,920 B to 20 allocations /
    1,520 B, and raw executor task registration remains 0.9% below the
    original instruction baseline.
    
    `ExecutorBuilder::build()` is safe. Dropping an executor synchronously
    cancels remaining tasks. Join handles and cloned wakers may outlive
    executor task storage. Arty keeps its own public `JoinError` and maps
    internal executor errors at the crate boundary.
    
    ## Safe executor contract
    
    - `ExecutorBuilder::build()` is safe.
    - Direct `Executor` drop cancels and releases remaining task storage.
    `begin_shutdown()` remains available when a low-level owner needs
    explicit shutdown control.
    - `TaskSet::add(future, options)` requires explicit `TaskOptions` and
    returns `Result<R, arty_executor::JoinError>` through its join handle.
    - Submissions after shutdown or executor drop return an immediately
    ready shutdown error instead of panicking.
    - Join handles retain independent result storage and may outlive the
    executor. Dropping a join does not cancel its task.
    - Shutdown timeout configuration and the old requirement to drop every
    join before shutdown are removed.
    
    ## Task completion and panic delivery
    
    - Plain local executor tasks remain fail-fast on panic.
    - Arty creates concrete, potentially non-`Send` futures on their
    destination worker while returning `Send` result handles.
    - Cross-thread result transport lives under `arty_executor::remote`.
    Adopting runtimes store a `Pool` and rent `Sender`/`JoinHandle` pairs
    whose storage is reused after both endpoints are dropped.
    - `arty_executor::remote::Sender` contains panics from receiver
    notification even when a disconnected or shutting-down executor rejects
    the submission before consuming the sender.
    - Arty's local public `JoinError` converts executor errors through a
    crate-private helper. `into_panic()` is public and returns `Option`, so
    shutdown has no panic precondition.
    - Cancellation reports a task-panic event when the future destructor
    panics. A panicking result notification is contained without being
    misclassified as a task panic.
    
    ## Task options and telemetry
    
    `TaskOptions` carries optional `observed` context and scheduling
    placement. A configured sink receives lifecycle telemetry even with
    `TaskOptions::default()`; context enrichment remains optional.
    
    - `arty.rt.task.spawned`, with `placement = any|same_thread`
    - `arty.rt.task.succeeded`, with aggregate poll count and unit `{poll}`
    - `arty.rt.task.panicked`, with aggregate poll count and unit `{poll}`
    
    Spawn telemetry uses an independently owned context and sink snapshot.
    External processors run after the executor releases state without
    retaining a raw task reference, so they may submit work or execute a
    cycle that completes the new task.
    
    ## Shutdown and wake correctness
    
    - `RuntimeOperations::request_stop()` asks the current worker's
    `TaskSet` to begin shutdown reentrantly before publishing runtime-wide
    shutdown.
    - Runtime-owned deferred futures check the shared shutdown state on
    their first poll before invoking user factories. The check stays in Arty
    rather than coupling the executor to runtime state.
    - The executor checks its local shutdown state before every remaining
    active-task poll. Deferred same-worker and remote factories after the
    stopping task are cancelled without invocation and their joins resolve
    as shutdown.
    - External-thread, owner-stop, and owner-drop shutdown are observed by
    every deferred runtime factory through the shared runtime state.
    - New and inactive tasks are cancelled in the same cycle after local
    shutdown begins.
    - A wake that falls back to embedded state while an active task is being
    reclassified is reprobed on the next cycle.
    - Executor drop always finishes cancellation, including request-only
    shutdown followed by immediate drop.
    
    ## Cloned-waker contract
    
    1. A cloned waker may be invoked from any thread. While its task is
    pending, the notification is routed to the worker that owns and polls
    that task.
    2. Completion and cancellation disconnect copied wakers from task memory
    before that memory is reused.
    3. Calling a copied waker after completion, cancellation, or shutdown is
    safe and has no effect.
    4. Waker copies do not own the task, worker, or runtime and cannot delay
    shutdown.
    
    The wake state uses reusable pooled storage and counted ownership. This
    preserves allocation-free warmed registration while preventing copied
    wakers from extending task lifetime.
    
    ## Breaking changes
    
    - Remove `unsafe` around `ExecutorBuilder::build()`.
    - Pass `TaskOptions::default()` to `TaskSet::add` when no integration
    context is needed.
    - Handle `Result<R, arty_executor::JoinError>` when awaiting a raw
    executor join.
    - Arty users continue to receive `arty::task::JoinError`.
    - Replace root `RemoteJoinHandle`/`RemoteTaskSender` usage with
    `arty_executor::remote::{Pool, JoinHandle, Sender}` and retain the pool
    to reuse channel storage.
    - Remove deleted shutdown-timeout builder options.
    - Do not wait for joins before dropping an executor. Retained handles
    resolve independently.
    - The public `test-util` feature and `arty_executor::testing` module are
    removed. Tests and benchmarks should use `Executor::builder()`,
    `TaskSet`, joins, cycles, and wakers through supported public APIs.
    - `testing::new_guarded_executor` has no replacement because executor
    construction is safe. Tests should assert behavior rather than the
    private `AWAKENED_CAPACITY` value.
    
    ## Audit, packaging, and macro follow-ups
    
    - `docs/arty-runtime-panic-audit-disposition.md` revalidates #800
    against the post-#785 baseline while preserving distinctions among
    production panics, deadlocks, invariants, debug checks, and
    non-production findings.
    - `arty_macros_impl` resolves Cargo-renamed `arty` dependencies.
    Resolver coverage remains in the implementation crate.
    - Arty's `ohno` dev-dependency is path-only.
    - The package preface remains `Single-threaded, thread-aware application
    runtime.` Runtime docs separately explain multi-worker size and task
    placement.
    - `ae_minimal` is the sole executor example. Safe executor drop handles
    shutdown implicitly.
    - Generated README files are regenerated only through Anvil.
    
    ## Azure DevOps
    
    - [Feature 7852437: Arty API
    stable-ready](https://dev.azure.com/O365Exchange/O365%20Core/_workitems/edit/7852437)
    - [User Story 8026956: Decide independent waker behavior for
    arty](https://dev.azure.com/O365Exchange/O365%20Core/_workitems/edit/8026956)
    
