    HTTP decompression defaults: 64 MiB output and 1,024 compressed streams
    per decompression stage
    - have `fetch` inherit those defaults unchanged and forward only
    explicit caller overrides
    - preserve explicit bounds and `unbounded_output_len()` /
    `unbounded_streams()` opt-outs through
    `DecompressorLimits::with_fallbacks`
    - document the HTTP policies and explicit opt-out contracts in both
    `http_compression` and `fetch`
    - move 26 `compressors` tests that only exercise supported API into
    integration-test targets, while retaining private unit coverage for the
    raw-DEFLATE runtime guard
    
    ## Scanner findings
    
    ### M2 — confirmed defect / API-safety gap
    
    `CompressionLayer` built a streaming decompressor from
    `DecompressorLimits::new()`. That is intentionally unbounded in
    cumulative output because the lower-level streaming API retains only one
    bounded chunk at a time, but HTTP peers are untrusted and can make the
    consumer process an arbitrarily large expanded body or decode
    arbitrarily many empty concatenated members.
    
    The layer now supplies 64 MiB cumulative-output and
    1,024-stream-per-stage fallbacks. Caller-provided fields win; fields
    left unset inherit the HTTP defaults; removing either cap requires the
    explicit `unbounded_output_len()` or `unbounded_streams()` API. `fetch`
    adds no default limit overrides of its own. Public-API integration tests
    verify default rejection at both boundaries and prove finite raises and
    explicit opt-outs are honored.
    
    ### M6 — false positive on current code
    
    `Pump::pull` does not blindly trust arbitrary safe code. `Codec` is a
    crate-private `unsafe trait`; its safety contract requires
    `output[..produced]` to be initialized. `Pump::pull` separately rejects
    `consumed > supplied` and `produced > provided_output` before calling
    unsafe `BytesBuf::advance`. Existing regression coverage deliberately
    over-reports output and verifies that no uninitialized bytes are
    advanced. Backend implementations use `flate2` uninitialized-output
    APIs, `zstd_safe::WriteBuf`, or fully initialize brotli's output slice.
    
    No engine change is appropriate: runtime code cannot inspect whether
    bytes are initialized, and replacing the audited unsafe boundary with
    unconditional zero-filling would add hot-path work without strengthening
    the contract.
    
    ### Read-timeout/defaults claim — not a compressor defect
    
    Read/idle timeout policy belongs to `http_extensions::HttpBodyOptions`,
    not `compressors` or `http_compression`. `CompressionLayer` uses
    `HttpBodyBuilder::rewrap`, which preserves and continues enforcing the
    source body's timeout. It deliberately does not add a second timeout
    around transformed frames, because a decompressor may consume many
    timely source frames before producing one output frame. Changing the
    global `None` default here would be unrelated and could create false
    timeouts.
    
    ## Safety guarantees after this change
    
    - default HTTP decompression cannot emit more than 64 MiB or decode more
    than 1,024 compressed streams per decompression stage
    - `fetch` inherits the same defaults instead of duplicating policy
    - tightening one decompressor field does not accidentally discard either
    HTTP fallback
    - finite raises and explicit unbounded opt-outs remain available and
    visible at the call site
    - lower-level streaming decompression remains suitable for arbitrarily
    long streams with bounded working memory when used directly
    - codec-produced counts are range-checked before unsafe advancement,
    with initialization guaranteed by the sealed unsafe trait
    
