    refactor(arty_io_core)!: option 3 - simplified driver contract (#809)
    
    ## Option 3: simplified driver contract
    
    This option lets `IoContext` create worker-local drivers directly,
    removing the separate provider, runtime-owned pending-work APIs, and
    blocking-work scheduler contract.
    
    ## Owner flow
    
    1. The owner creates the application-facing context with
    `IoContext::context(options)`, using the context's associated
    `ContextOptions` type.
    2. It clones or relocates that context to each worker and calls
    `IoContext::driver(DriverOptions)`.
    3. Creation returns `DriverInstance { driver, role }`; the owner stores
    the driver and publishes the context to application code.
    4. The owner calls `Driver::execute_cycle`. The driver uses the supplied
    owner waker to request another cycle, while `Driver::waker` lets the
    owner interrupt a pending driver wait.
    5. Shutdown consumes the driver through `Driver::shutdown`.
    
    ## Public API
    
    ```rust
    pub trait IoContext: Clone + ThreadAware + 'static {
        type ContextOptions;
        type Driver: Driver;
    
        fn context(options: Self::ContextOptions) -> Self;
        fn driver(&mut self, options: DriverOptions)
            -> Result<DriverInstance<Self::Driver>, DriverError>;
    }
    
    pub trait Driver: 'static {
        fn waker(&self) -> Waker;
        fn execute_cycle(&mut self, cycle: &mut Cycle) -> Result<(), DriverError>;
        fn shutdown(self) -> Result<(), DriverError> where Self: Sized;
    }
    
    #[non_exhaustive]
    pub struct DriverInstance<D> {
        pub driver: D,
        pub role: DriverRole,
    }
    ```
    
    `DriverOptions` supplies only the owning worker, owner waker, and
    `allowed_roles`. The context chooses a role and returns it with the
    driver. Blocking execution remains an implementation concern rather than
    a shared runtime contract.
    
    Validation: `cargo check -p arty_io_core --all-targets`.
    
    Standalone against `main`.
    
