    feat(metabench): add Intel VTune support alongside Linux perf (#750)
    
    ## Summary
    
    Adds a `Mode::Vtune` probe mode integrated the same way as the existing
    Linux `perf` mode: it measures exactly the annotated Criterion workload
    for every discovered benchmark case via a new `vtune.rs` control module.
    
    Unlike `perf`'s FIFO-based enable/disable protocol, VTune's CLI exposes
    external pause/resume as its own synchronous subcommands (`vtune
    -command resume|pause -r <result-dir>`), so the worker is launched with
    `--start-paused` and the annotated function's `begin()`/`Drop` guard
    resumes/pauses the collection directly instead of polling a FIFO.
    Results are extracted with `vtune -report hw-events -format csv` and
    parsed into the same `NativeResult` shape `perf` produces, so they flow
    through the existing report/baseline/regression machinery unchanged.
    
    VTune is available on both Linux and Windows (gated on the `vtune`
    binary itself rather than `target_os`), and is opt-in only via `--vtune`
    / `BENCH_ENGINE=vtune`, mirroring `perf`'s exclusion from the default
    engine set.
    
    ## Testing
    
    - Argument-validation and CSV-parsing unit tests, including short-alias
    rejection (`-C`/`-R`/`-c`) and thousands-separated hardware-event
    counts.
    - A unit test covering `finish_worker`'s "workload not measured" failure
    path.
    - Fake-`vtune`-script integration tests exercising the full
    worker/control/report pipeline end-to-end, including a control-protocol
    failure path that surfaces `Error::VtuneControl`.
    
    Verified with `cargo test -p metabench` (lib + integration), `just
    clippy`, `just format`, `just spellcheck`, and `just readme`.
    
