    feat(thread_aware_core): add the stable core crate for thread-aware state (#643)
    
    ## Summary
    
    Adds `thread_aware_core`, a small, dependency-free vocabulary crate for
    values that adapt when a runtime moves them between threads.
    
    ## Public API
    
    - `ThreadAware: Send` defines `relocate(source: Option<&Thread>,
    destination: &Thread)`, an advisory callback invoked after a value
    moves. Implementations must preserve correctness when relocation is
    skipped or repeated, must not panic or block, and may use the callback
    only to improve placement or performance.
    - `Thread` describes where a value runs through three coordinates: the
    owning runtime (`Owner`), the OS thread (`std::thread::ThreadId`), and
    the nearest memory region (`NumaNode`). Its fields are private and
    exposed through accessors.
    - `Owner` values have process-unique identities, allowing
    implementations to detect movement between runtimes.
    - `NumaNode` is an opaque identifier for memory locality.
    - Runtime integration uses the doc-hidden, versioned `__private::v1`
    constructors, keeping construction plumbing outside the
    downstream-facing stable surface.
    
    ## Provided implementations
    
    The crate provides no-op implementations for primitives and other values
    with no thread-bound state, and forwarding implementations for common
    composed types, including `PhantomData`, tuples, arrays, slices,
    `Option`, `Result`, `Vec`, `VecDeque`, `Box`, `BTreeMap`, and, with
    `std`, `HashMap`. Map keys are not relocated.
    
    References, sets, `Cow`, and `Arc` intentionally have no blanket
    implementation because relocation would be ambiguous or could violate
    their invariants. Policy-bearing wrappers and derive support belong in
    the `thread_aware` utility crate.
    
    ## Features and compatibility
    
    - The default `std` feature adds the OS thread coordinate and
    implementations for standard-library types such as `HashMap`, `Path`,
    and `PathBuf`.
    - With default features disabled, the crate supports `no_std` with
    `alloc` and pointer-width atomics. `Thread` retains `Owner` and
    `NumaNode`, while construction and `Thread::id` remain `std`-only
    runtime concerns.
    - The crate has no normal dependencies; `static_assertions` is used only
    for tests.
    - Static assertions pin the expected auto traits and dyn compatibility
    of `ThreadAware`, while unit tests, doctests, and the external `no_std`
    surface test cover the public contract.
    
    ## Scope
    
    This PR adds the crate and its workspace/package wiring.
    `thread_aware_core` is currently standalone; the existing `thread_aware`
    crate continues to expose its own API, and adoption of the core
    vocabulary is outside this PR.
    
