    feat(msvc_spectre_libs): add crate with build-system override for Spectre CRT discovery (#649)
    
    ## Summary
    
    Adds the `msvc_spectre_libs` crate to the workspace as a first-class
    member (v0.2.0). Adding it as a **dependency** makes Windows MSVC
    targets link against the Spectre-mitigated (`/Qspectre`) CRT import
    libraries automatically; it is a no-op on every other target.
    
    It must be a normal `[dependencies]` entry, not a build-dependency: a
    build-dependency lives in the *host* build graph, so its link-search
    path would apply only to the consumer's own build script rather than to
    the shipped artifact.
    
    Contract and rationale are recorded in
    [`docs/design.md`](https://github.com/microsoft/oxidizer/blob/main/crates/msvc_spectre_libs/docs/design.md);
    the implementation strategy is in
    [`docs/implementation.md`](https://github.com/microsoft/oxidizer/blob/main/crates/msvc_spectre_libs/docs/implementation.md).
    
    ## Design: override-first resolution
    
    The build script resolves the Spectre library directory from these
    sources, in this order:
    
    1. **Build-system override (preferred).** If
    `MSVC_SPECTRE_LIB_DIR_<triple>` (e.g.
    `MSVC_SPECTRE_LIB_DIR_x86_64_pc_windows_msvc`) or the target-agnostic
    `MSVC_SPECTRE_LIB_DIR` points at an existing directory, it is used
    verbatim. This lets a repository or CI system that already knows the
    toolchain layout (e.g. one that provisions the MSVC libraries from a
    package feed) supply the exact path without any registry probing. An
    override that does not exist is a diagnostic; it never silently falls
    through to discovery.
    2. **Toolchain root from the environment.** Otherwise, if
    `VCToolsInstallDir` is set — by a developer command prompt or by
    `vcvars` — the libraries are `lib\spectre\<arch>` directly beneath it,
    with no parent-directory climbing relative to `cl.exe`.
    3. **Registry discovery (fallback).** Otherwise the script locates
    `cl.exe` via the Windows registry (`cc::windows_registry`) and derives
    `lib\spectre\<arch>` from its toolchain root.
    
    Gating is on `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_OS` / `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ENV` rather than
    `cfg!`, because a build script is compiled for and runs on the *host* —
    so `cfg!` would describe the wrong machine in both cross-compilation
    directions.
    
    Pure, unit-tested helpers cover the naming and mapping rules:
    `resolve::SpectreArch` (a `#[non_exhaustive]` enum mapping
    `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ARCH` values to the toolchain's directory names),
    `resolve::override_var_name`, `resolve::spectre_lib_dir`, and
    `flags::required_link_args_var_name`. They live in the companion package
    described below.
    
    ## Two packages: policy and adapter
    
    The work is split so that every rule worth testing is a pure function,
    and everything that touches the world is a thin adapter over it.
    
    - **`msvc_spectre_libs`** is what a consumer depends on, and the only
    package with a build-time effect. It exposes **no public Rust API at
    all** — its library target carries documentation only — so nothing about
    the mechanism is frozen into a versioned surface that consumers could
    come to rely on. `build.rs` is ~35 lines: capture the environment, call
    the planner, print the directives, and (under `error`) exit non-zero.
    - **`msvc_spectre_libs_build`** is the build-time companion package,
    following the `routerama_build` precedent already in the workspace.
    `plan::plan` takes a `BuildEnvironment` (a plain struct of already-read
    values) and a `&dyn Toolchain`, and returns a `Plan`: the
    `rerun-if-env-changed` variables, the link-search directories, and the
    diagnostics. It prints nothing, reads nothing, and exits nothing.
    
    `Toolchain` is a two-method port because exactly two inputs cannot be
    captured up front — whether a candidate directory exists, and where
    `cl.exe` is for the target — and both must stay lazy so that a build
    whose override already answered the question never probes the registry.
    Tests supply a fake whose answers come from the table.
    
    Environment reads are captured as a three-state `EnvValue` (`Absent` /
    `NotUnicode` / `Present`) rather than an `Option`, so "set but
    unreadable" is data the planner decides on rather than something
    resolved during the read. A `cargo:` directive is a UTF-8 text line, so
    a non-Unicode path is a hard diagnostic: emitting it lossily would point
    the linker at a directory other than the one that was validated, and
    treating it as unset would fall through to discovery and quietly link
    the *unmitigated* CRT.
    
    Diagnostics are `ohno::AppError`; `Display` renders the message alone,
    so `cargo:warning=` text is unchanged. The split also replaced the
    earlier `#[path]` sharing between the library and its build script, so
    each file is now compiled once and owned by one package, and the
    `unreachable_pub` suppression that existed only to paper over the second
    compilation is gone.
    
    The directory is published with `cargo:rustc-link-search`, which
    **propagates** through the target graph to the final link — unlike
    `cargo:rustc-link-arg`, which applies only to the artifacts of the
    package that emits it. That asymmetry is what makes a search path the
    right delivery mechanism here, and it is also why the crate cannot
    deliver hardening *linker arguments* on a consumer's behalf (see below).
    
    The directive is emitted unconditionally for an existing directory. An
    explicit `-L` search path is consulted *before* the `LIB` environment
    variable and in the order given, so suppressing it because the same
    directory appears somewhere in `LIB` could leave an ordinary CRT
    directory ahead of the mitigated one. A duplicate search path costs the
    linker nothing.
    
    ## Required-linker-argument verification
    
    Hardening arguments such as `/CETCOMPAT` cannot be delivered by this
    crate at all, for the `rustc-link-arg` reason above: they must come from
    `.cargo/config.toml` or `RUSTFLAGS`.
    
    That creates a silent-failure mode. A `RUSTFLAGS` environment variable
    **replaces** — it does not merge with — `target.<triple>.rustflags` from
    `.cargo/config.toml`. So an unrelated ambient `RUSTFLAGS` drops every
    argument configured there, and the build still succeeds, producing a
    binary that quietly fails its security requirements.
    
    The build script inspects `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` and reports any
    required argument that did not reach `rustc`, turning that silent drop
    into a build diagnostic. The requirement list comes from
    `MSVC_SPECTRE_REQUIRED_LINK_ARGS_<triple>`, or the target-agnostic
    `MSVC_SPECTRE_REQUIRED_LINK_ARGS`, with the target-suffixed form taking
    precedence:
    
    ```toml
    # .cargo/config.toml
    [env]
    MSVC_SPECTRE_REQUIRED_LINK_ARGS_x86_64_pc_windows_msvc = "/CETCOMPAT"
    
    [target.x86_64-pc-windows-msvc]
    rustflags = ["-Clink-arg=/CETCOMPAT"]
    ```
    
    Prefer the target-suffixed variable for anything architecture-specific,
    as `/CETCOMPAT` is: the `[env]` table applies to every selected target,
    so a target-agnostic requirement would be reported as missing on the
    targets whose `rustflags` cannot carry it.
    
    The check is **off by default**, so the crate imposes no policy of its
    own: which arguments are required depends on the toolchain in use and on
    the compliance requirements integrators must meet. Setting it under
    `[env]` in `.cargo/config.toml`, rather than in the ambient environment,
    keeps every entry point into the build in agreement about the value —
    which matters for build systems that can be driven both by `cargo`
    directly and by an outer driver such as MSBuild.
    
    Listing an argument the toolchain *already* emits (commonly `/guard:cf`,
    `/DYNAMICBASE`, `/HIGHENTROPYVA`, `/NXCOMPAT`) would demand a redundant
    second copy of a flag that is already in effect; the docs point
    integrators at `dumpbin /headers` to check first.
    
    The parsing and comparison logic lives in a `flags` module as pure
    functions over already-captured environment strings, so it is unit
    tested without touching the environment. It accepts every spelling
    `rustc` recognizes — `-Clink-arg=X`, the split `-C` `link-arg=X`, and
    `-Clink-args="X Y"` — so a correctly configured build is never
    misdiagnosed. `Absent` disables the check, but a value that is not valid
    Unicode becomes a diagnostic rather than a silent skip of a check the
    integrator opted in to.
    
    ## Assurance boundary
    
    Both checks inspect the **configuration** of a build: whether a search
    path resolved, and whether the configured arguments appear in the flags
    Cargo reports to `rustc`. Neither observes the linker invocation, and
    neither inspects the produced binary. A successful build — including
    with `error` enabled — establishes that the build was configured to link
    the mitigated runtime, not that the artifact provably did. The
    documentation states this explicitly and directs readers to `dumpbin
    /headers` where artifact-level evidence is required.
    
    ## Features
    
    - `error`: turns build diagnostics — Spectre libraries not found, or a
    required linker argument missing from `rustc` — into hard build errors,
    for builds that must not silently ship an unmitigated binary.
    
    Both checks run even when the other has already failed, so one build
    surfaces every problem rather than one per iteration.
    
    ## Workspace integration
    
    - First-class workspace member: no `exclude`, no `.cargo-heather.toml`
    exclusion, standard `cargo sort --workspace`.
    - Two first-class workspace members. `cc` added to
    `[workspace.dependencies]`; `msvc_spectre_libs` declares
    `msvc_spectre_libs_build` as an unconditional `[build-dependencies]`
    entry (target-scoped build-dependencies are evaluated against the
    *host*, so a Linux host cross-compiling to Windows MSVC would not select
    it). A `cargo-machete` ignore covers it, since machete does not scan
    build scripts, mirroring the `fetch` crate.
    - README generated from the crate docs via `cargo-doc2readme`.
    - `.spelling` additions.
    
    ## Validation (local)
    
    `fmt` (local + CI `unstable-rustfmt.toml`), `cargo-sort`,
    `cargo-machete`, `clippy` (default + `--all-features`), `doc2readme
    --check`, `spellcheck`, `nextest` (33 unit + 9 doc tests), `cargo
    mutants` (46 mutants, 43 caught, 3 unviable, 0 missed, 0 timeouts), and
    a `--locked` build all pass. The `error` feature stays green on CI: both
    `windows-latest` (Server 2025) and `windows-11-arm` runner images
    provision `VC.Runtimes.*.Spectre`, and the required-argument check is
    inert unless a requirement variable is set.
    
