    fix(thread_aware_macros): bound phantom-only generics by Send (#678)
    
    &#x1F916; This description was written by an AI agent.
    
    ## Problem
    
    `#[derive(ThreadAware)]` fails to compile for any type whose generic
    parameter
    appears only inside a `PhantomData` field:
    
    ```rust
    #[derive(ThreadAware)]
    struct DirectPhantom<T, U>(T, PhantomData<U>);
    ```
    
    ```text
    error[E0277]: `U` cannot be sent between threads safely
    note: required because it appears within the type `PhantomData<U>`
    note: required by a bound in `ThreadAware`
    ```
    
    `collect_generics_in_type` returned early on `PhantomData`, so `U`
    gained no
    bound at all and the generated impl could not satisfy the `ThreadAware:
    Send`
    supertrait. Skipping the field in the relocation body is correct —
    `PhantomData`
    holds no value to relocate — but skipping it also dropped the only bound
    that
    would have made the type `Send`.
    
    A related shape failed for a different reason: `is_phantom_data` is a
    syntactic
    check on the field type, so a nested occurrence such as
    `(PhantomData<T>,)` was
    relocated like any other field, and no `ThreadAware` impl for
    `PhantomData`
    existed to satisfy it.
    
    The only workaround was to widen the user's own public API to satisfy
    the derive:
    
    ```rust
    struct Workaround<T, U: Send>(T, PhantomData<U>);
    ```
    
    ## Change
    
    Generic parameters are now classified by how they are reached from the
    fields:
    
    - reachable through a relocated field — bound by `ThreadAware`, as
    before;
    - named only inside `PhantomData` — bound by `Send`.
    
    The distinction matters. Binding phantom-only parameters by
    `ThreadAware` would
    over-restrict: `std::sync::Arc<i32>` is `Send` but deliberately not
    `ThreadAware`, and `PhantomData<Arc<i32>>` is a legitimate field type.
    
    Adds `impl<T: ?Sized + Send> ThreadAware for PhantomData<T>`, so a
    `PhantomData`
    nested in a tuple, array or reference relocates as a no-op. This also
    covers
    hand-written generic code, not just derived impls.
    
    Five snapshots changed. Each records only the `impl<...>` bound line,
    and each
    previously recorded the defect — `derive__generics_add_bounds.snap` held
    exactly
    the `impl<T: ThreadAware, U>` shape that fails to compile.
    
    ## Validation
    
    ```text
    cargo test -p thread_aware -p thread_aware_macros_impl   185 passed, 0 failed
    just package=thread_aware clippy                         clean
    just package=thread_aware_macros_impl clippy             clean
    just package=thread_aware format                         no changes
    ```
    
    Six compile-and-run cases were added to
    `crates/thread_aware/tests/derive_compiles.rs`,
    covering phantom-only generics, nested `PhantomData`, a concrete
    `PhantomData<Arc<i32>>`, `#[thread_aware(skip)]`, and an enum carrying a
    phantom-only parameter. Two assert behaviour rather than compilation:
    that
    `relocate` reaches every non-skipped field exactly once and no skipped
    one.
    
    To confirm these are genuine regression tests, the two source fixes were
    stashed
    and the suite re-run against the new tests. It reproduced both reported
    errors
    verbatim — ``U` cannot be sent between threads safely` and `the trait
    bound
    `PhantomData<T>: ThreadAware` is not satisfied` — and compiled again
    once the
    fixes were restored.
    
    Nine further cases pin the bound-selection logic itself, added after the
    coverage
    gate and mutation testing flagged gaps: a phantom-only generic already
    bound by
    `Send` or `ThreadAware` (no duplicate bound), a declared-but-unused
    parameter (no
    bound at all), lifetime and const-generic parameters (skipped), and a
    generic
    reachable only through a reference, tuple, array, parenthesized or
    grouped type
    inside `PhantomData`. Measured over the affected package set,
    `thread_aware_macros_impl/src/lib.rs` is at 100% lines, and mutation
    testing over
    that file reports 42 of 42 viable mutants caught.
    
    ## Reviewer notes
    
    The derive was previously covered only by `insta` snapshots, which
    compare token
    streams and never compile them. A snapshot can confirm the output
    matches what
    was recorded, but here the recorded output was itself the defect, so the
    suite
    passed while the feature was unusable. The new tests link the real crate
    and run
    the expansion, which is why they catch this class of bug.
    
    Snapshots remain useful for reviewing expansion diffs and are kept.
    
    Out of scope, worth a follow-up: `trybuild` cases pinning the
    diagnostics for
    shapes that must keep failing (such as unions), and an audit of the
    other derive
    macros in the workspace for the same snapshot-only coverage pattern.
    
