    perf(thread_aware)!: partition relocation storage per affinity (#681)
    
    [Copilot speaking]
    
    ## Motivation
    
    `thread_aware::Arc<T, S>::relocate` is called on every cross-affinity
    handoff for every thread-aware `Arc` reachable in the relocated object
    graph. It previously took an unconditional exclusive `RwLock` write
    lock, including the common case where the destination value was already
    materialized. Read-only relocations therefore serialized through one
    table-wide lock. (Azure DevOps 7747020.)
    
    ## Changes
    
    **Strategy-partitioned write-once storage.** A strategy maps each
    affinity into a strategy partition. The table is sized once behind a
    `OnceLock`, and each partition owns an independent
    `OnceLock<sync::Arc<T>>`.
    
    - The hit path reads the destination cell with a plain acquire load and
    carries no cell or table lock-word contention. Adopting and releasing
    `sync::Arc` values still update their strong counts, so callers that
    share an allocation can contend on its reference count.
    - The miss path uses `get_or_init`, so one racer materializes the
    destination partition and every other racer adopts the published value.
    The factory runs at most once per strategy partition.
    
    **Relocation correctness.** A cross-partition miss records the value the
    `Arc` moved away from in the source partition with a write-once `set`,
    allowing later relocation back to recover it without holding multiple
    cells simultaneously. Relocation within one strategy partition keeps the
    carried value, which preserves grouped strategies such as `PerProcess`
    and `PerNuma`.
    
    **Initialization behavior.** Constructor functions, clone functions, and
    captured `ThreadAware` state run while the destination cell is
    initializing. They must not reenter that cell or create cyclic
    initialization dependencies. A panic propagates and leaves the cell
    empty for a later relocation to retry; there is no poisonable lock or
    partially published value.
    
    **Out-of-range affinities.** Relocation to an affinity outside the
    table's coordinate space is a no-op that keeps the carried value and
    records the `thread_aware_arc_oob` event. Direct `Storage::insert` and
    `Storage::get` calls remain strict and panic when their affinity is out
    of range.
    
    **Constructible storage handle.** The public `storage::Storage<T, S>`
    exposes affinity-keyed write-once insertion and lookup without exposing
    the internal cell layout. `Storage::insert` returns `Ok(())` when it
    publishes a value and `Err(value)` when the selected partition is
    already populated.
    
    **API terminology.** `Arc`, `Storage`, `Strategy`, the built-in
    strategies, and the crate documentation consistently describe value
    identity and materialization in terms of strategy partitions. Affinity
    terminology is reserved for the source and destination coordinates that
    strategies map into those partitions.
    
    **Benchmarks.** The unpublished `thread_aware_benchmarking` package owns
    the shared five-layer object-tree model and both benchmark harnesses
    without compiling shared code through `#[path]`.
    
    - Criterion measures single-threaded hit and miss paths plus saturated
    and oversubscribed concurrent relocation.
    - Each measured hit starts from a value resident in the declared source
    partition.
    - Concurrent workers alternate between private, already-populated source
    and destination partitions after a barrier release, preserving the
    `ThreadAware` caller contract without introducing cross-worker cell
    contention.
    - The paired Gungraun suite measures the same single-threaded hit and
    miss operations under Callgrind.
    
    ## Structure
    
    ```text
    Arc<T, S> clones -- share --> Storage<T, S>              (public, opaque handle)
                                      |
                                      +-- SlotTable: OnceLock<[ OnceLock<Arc<T>> ; N ]>
                                                              partition 0  ...  partition N-1
                                                              (sized once; published once)
    ```
    
