    feat(internity): export frozen readers and guarantee dense handles (#657)
    
    Addresses a field report from a production XML stack that adopted
    `internity` as its string interner, replacing `lasso`. Each change below
    pairs the fix with the problem it solves.
    
    ## Export the frozen readers as concrete types
    
    `freeze` returned an unnameable `impl Reader`, so a struct wanting to
    *hold* a frozen table had no way to write the field type and had to fall
    back to `Box<dyn Reader>` — an allocation plus virtual dispatch on the
    hottest read path, with no inlining through it.
    
    Both readers are now public and returned concretely, renamed to match
    their lexicons:
    
    | Before | After |
    |---|---|
    | `pub(crate) struct FlatReader` | `pub struct LocalReader` |
    | `pub(crate) struct ShardedReader` | `pub struct ThreadedReader` |
    | `LocalLexicon::freeze(self) -> impl Reader` |
    `LocalLexicon::freeze(self) -> LocalReader` |
    | `ThreadedLexicon::freeze(self) -> impl Reader` |
    `ThreadedLexicon::freeze(self) -> ThreadedReader` |
    
    **Not a breaking change**: a concrete return type still satisfies code
    written against `impl Reader`, and the old names were never public.
    Renaming was free now and would have been breaking later. It also
    removes a near-collision with the existing internal `ShardReader` (one
    shard, versus all of them).
    
    `Reader` remains sealed, so downstream crates still cannot implement it.
    That is the only guarantee sealing gives us: publishing these types
    *does* widen the supported surface, since their names, inherent methods,
    and auto-trait bounds all become compatibility commitments. That is the
    intended trade — being nameable is the point.
    
    ## Guarantee dense handles, and add safe conversions
    
    Downstream code was already deriving a dense index from `as_u32() - 1` —
    an implementation detail we never promised to keep. Handles being
    consecutive is what makes a `Vec<T>` side table possible instead of a
    hash map keyed by the handle.
    
    Adds `index_of` and `sym_at` to `LocalLexicon` and `LocalReader`, both
    returning `Option`, and documents consecutive insertion-order numbering
    as a guarantee that `freeze` preserves.
    
    They live on the lexicon and reader rather than on `Sym` deliberately:
    `Sym` does not know which interner produced it, so a method there could
    not range-check, and `ThreadedLexicon` handles — which pack a shard
    index into their high bits and are **not** dense — could reach it by
    mistake. Putting the pair here means the conversion simply is not
    reachable when you only hold a `ThreadedReader`. Note this is about
    which types expose the method, not about which handles can reach it:
    `Sym` is one shared type, so a foreign handle that happens to be in
    range still returns a valid-but-wrong index. The docs say so, and say to
    keep side tables with the lexicon they describe.
    
    `as_u32` / `from_u32` are unchanged: they are the opaque round-trip that
    works for *both* interners, which `index_of` cannot replace.
    
    ## Document the trade-offs on the types
    
    The CSR layout costs 4 bytes per string where a `Vec<&str>` design costs
    16, in exchange for one extra dependent load per resolve. That is a good
    trade, but a reader comparing against `lasso` should see it stated
    plainly rather than discover it.
    
    The hash-DoS warning also moves onto the types themselves. It previously
    lived only in module-level prose, and there was nothing anywhere noting
    that `lasso` defaults to a collision-resistant hasher — so a
    type-for-type migration silently lost that protection.
    
    ## Document rebuilding a string lookup over a frozen reader
    
    Freezing drops the dedup hash map, which *is* the memory saving, so a
    frozen reader can resolve but not look strings up. A sorted handle list
    costs ~4 bytes per string against the ~7 the hash map costs and answers
    lookups in `O(log n)`.
    
    This needs no new API — `Reader::iter` and `Reader::resolve` are enough
    — so it ships as a doctest on `freeze` rather than as crate code.
    Building it in would have added permanent public API surface to save
    callers about 18 lines, with an unmeasured chance that bisection loses
    to simply keeping the hash map.
    
    ## Testing
    
    - 5 new integration tests: index round-trip through lexicon *and*
    reader, out-of-range handling, a dense side table, storing both readers
    by value in a struct, and the external lookup pattern end to end.
    - 4 new doctests, so the documented patterns cannot rot.
    - `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features`, tests under both
    `--all-features` and `--no-default-features`, format check, regenerated
    README, and spellcheck all pass locally. `just` itself could not run in
    this environment (its recipes require `pwsh`), so the underlying
    commands were invoked directly — CI is the real confirmation.
    
