    fix(ohno)!: document pub(crate) constructors, reject #[no_constructors] under #[ohno::error] (#653)
    
    Two related facts about the constructors `#[derive(Error)]` generates were undocumented, and the second turned out to be a design problem rather than a documentation one.
    
    ## 1. The generated constructors are `pub(crate)`
    
    `#[derive(Error)]` generates `new()` and `caused_by()` as `pub(crate)`
    (`ohno_macros/src/derive_error/constructors.rs`), but the documentation
    never said so. The
    generated-signature comments read `ConfigError::new(path: String) ->
    Self`, and the
    `#[no_constructors]` counter-example directly below writes `pub fn new`
    — both cues point the reader
    towards a public constructor.
    
    The ambiguity has a measured cost. On 2026-08-07, on the ox-sdk PR
    adopting `ohno` errors in
    `observed`, three independent AI reviewers posted the *same* incorrect
    finding: that
    `#[ohno::error]` generates public `new()` and `caused_by()`
    constructors. Three separate rebuttals
    for one missing sentence. Three models converging on the same wrong
    claim is not three coincidences
    — the reviewers were reading the docs correctly, and the docs were
    ambiguous.
    
    The docs now state the visibility, give the rationale (the owning crate
    keeps control of how its
    errors are built, so adding a field is not a breaking change for
    callers), and show the real
    expansion as the single `impl` block it is.
    
    ## 2. `#[no_constructors]` is now rejected under `#[ohno::error]`
    (breaking)
    
    Writing the first half surfaced the second. `#[ohno::error]` adds the
    `OhnoCore` field itself.
    `#[no_constructors]` removes the generated constructors, so the author
    has to write the struct
    literal by hand — which means naming that added field.
    
    That name is not theirs to rely on. `generate_unique_field_name` picks
    `ohno_core`, then
    `ohno_core_1`, `ohno_core_2` and so on if the struct already declares
    one. A hand-written
    constructor therefore names a field the macro chose, and an unrelated
    later field declaration can
    rename it out from under the constructor.
    
    **The one existing user is the evidence.** `cachet_tier::Error` was the
    only place in the repo
    combining the two:
    
    ```rust
    #[ohno::error]
    #[no_constructors]
    #[derive(Clone)]
    pub struct Error {
        recovery_info: RecoveryInfo,
    }
    
    impl Error {
        fn caused_by(cause: impl Into<Box<dyn StdError + Send + Sync>>) -> Self {
            Self {
                ohno_core: OhnoCore::from(cause),   // a field it never declared
                recovery_info: RecoveryInfo::never(),
            }
        }
    }
    ```
    
    Every other `#[no_constructors]` in `oxidizer` and `ox-sdk` already
    pairs with
    `#[derive(ohno::Error)]` (6 sites in ox-sdk, plus `http_extensions`,
    tests and examples here) and is
    unaffected.
    
    This is the rule the attribute already applies to `#[error]`, whose
    rejection tells the author to
    use `#[derive(ohno::Error)]` to place the core themselves. From
    `docs/error_error.md`: *"This
    attribute always adds the OhnoCore field itself. It always builds the
    error from the field it
    adds."* Building that value by hand is the same request, so it gets the
    same answer.
    
    The alternative — documenting the field name so manual construction is
    legitimate — was rejected: it
    would promise a name the macro reserves the right to change, turning an
    implementation detail into
    public API.
    
    ```
    error: `#[no_constructors]` is not supported under `#[ohno::error]`. Writing a constructor by hand
    means writing the struct literal, which needs the name of the OhnoCore field this attribute adds --
    a name it chooses, and changes if you later declare a field of your own called `ohno_core`. Use
    `#[derive(ohno::Error)]` and declare the OhnoCore field yourself, so the field your constructor
    names is one you can see
      --> tests/ui/ohno_error_no_constructors.rs:11:1
       |
    11 | #[no_constructors]
       | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    ```
    
    ## Changes
    
    - `ohno/src/lib.rs` — constructor visibility stated and rationalised;
    generated signatures shown as
    the `impl` block they actually are; `#[no_constructors]` documented as
    derive-only.
    - `ohno_macros/src/error_type_attr.rs` — `reject_no_constructors`,
    alongside the existing `reject_*`
      checks.
    - `ohno_macros/docs/error_error.md` — the rule under `#[ohno::error]`,
    plus an entry in "What gets
      rejected".
    - `ohno_macros/src/lib.rs` — the same notes on the `#[no_constructors]`
    bullet, since a reviewer may
      read the macro docs rather than the crate docs.
    - `cachet_tier/src/error.rs` — migrated to `#[derive(ohno::Error)]` with
    a declared `ohno_core`, so
      the field its constructors name is one a reader can see.
    - Tests: a unit test and a `trybuild` UI case, matching the sibling
    rejections.
    - Both READMEs regenerated with `cargo doc2readme`.
    
    ## Effects
    
    - A reader of either the crate docs or the macro docs learns the
    constructor visibility at the point
    where they would otherwise guess it, which should stop that class of
    review finding recurring.
    - Breaking for any out-of-tree crate combining `#[ohno::error]` with
    `#[no_constructors]`. The
    migration is mechanical — swap to `#[derive(ohno::Error)]` and declare
    the `OhnoCore` field — and
      the error message states it.
    - A struct that opts out of the generated constructors now declares its
    core, so the field a manual
    constructor names is visible at the definition instead of supplied by
    macro expansion.
    - `#[ohno::error]` covers one case fewer, and authors who want both
    behaviours write one extra
      field.
    
    ## Validation
    
    - `cargo +1.96.1 test -p ohno -p ohno_macros -p cachet_tier
    --all-features` — all pass, including
      the new UI case.
    - `just anvil-clippy` — clean across the workspace.
    - `rustfmt +nightly-2026-05-30 --config-path ./unstable-rustfmt.toml
    --check` — 0 would be
      reformatted.
    - `cargo doc2readme --check` — up to date for both `ohno` and
    `ohno_macros`.
    
