    chore: release bytesbuf v0.8.0 and 27 additional packages (#652)
    
    [Copilot speaking]
    
    ## Motivation
    
    Five unrelated breaking changes have accumulated since the last release
    and none of them have shipped. Because each affected crate is named in
    the `allowed_external_types` allowlist of its dependents, every
    dependent that re-exposes one of them must also go out as a breaking
    release for the published graph to stay coherent.
    
    ## Changes
    
    Six crates break on their own merit:
    
    - **`bytesbuf` 0.8.0** and **`thread_aware` 0.9.0** became `no_std`.
    Their hosted API moved behind a new `std` feature. `std` is on by
    default, so `default-features = true` consumers are unaffected, but
    builds using `default-features = false` must now add `std` to keep
    `bytesbuf::mem::GlobalPool`, `BytesBufWriter` and the `std::io`
    adapters, or `thread_aware`'s `Arc`, `PerCore`, `PerNuma`, `PerProcess`
    and `storage`. `thread_aware`'s `threads` feature now implies `std`.
    - **`multitude` 0.8.0** gained trait-object coercion: `Coercion`, the
    `coerce!` macro, `Box::unsize`, `Rc::unsize`, `Rc::unsize_pin`,
    `Arc::unsize` and `Arc::unsize_pin` convert an owner into a trait-object
    owner without moving or reallocating the value. Vtable metadata now
    lives in the handle rather than an allocation prefix, so trait-object
    handles are one word wider and the former "8 bytes for every `T`"
    guarantee holds only for sized values, `str` and `[T]`. The pointee
    bound on `Box`, `Rc`, `Arc`, `Cow` and the `Arena::alloc_dst_*` families
    tightened from `ptr_meta::Pointee` to the sealed `SmartPointerPointee`.
    - **`tick` 0.5.0** validates format values at construction and drops
    cross-format conversions, and adds the EcmaScript timestamp format.
    - **`ohno` 0.4.0** and **`ohno_macros` 0.4.0** tightened the
    `#[display]` and `#[error]` diagnostics. `#[display(...)]` arguments are
    validated before expansion, `#[error(generated)]` is rejected, combining
    `#[ohno::error]` with an `#[error]` marker is now a hard error, and a
    tuple index that addresses the injected core is refused. Inputs that
    previously expanded now fail to compile. `ohno`'s own library API is
    unchanged, but it re-exports the macros, so its effective contract moves
    with them.
    
    **`templated_uri` 0.4.0** and **`recoverable` 0.2.0** ship the
    documentation-module removal from #635, which took `_documentation` out
    of the normal build in four crates. `fetch` and `http_extensions` carry
    the same change, so releasing all four together keeps one change under
    one classification; deferring the other two would have left an
    unlabelled public-API removal waiting to reach consumers in a later
    patch release.
    
    **`templated_uri_macros` 0.4.0** and **`templated_uri_macros_impl`
    0.4.0** move in lockstep with the runtime crate they generate code
    against. `templated_uri` 0.3.6 introduced a version floor on the macro
    crate precisely so Cargo cannot unify macros across incompatible runtime
    lines; leaving the macros on 0.3 while `templated_uri` moves to 0.4
    would defeat that, and the published `_impl` crate would still resolve
    `ohno` 0.3, giving consumers two incompatible `ohno` copies.
    
    `plurality` 0.2.1 is additive only: unwind-safety impls for the pooled
    handles, plus a `coerce!` fix for trait objects that name an enclosing
    generic type parameter. `multitude_macros` and `multitude_macros_impl`
    0.1.1 pick up the generated README switch to absolute asset URLs so
    crates.io renders them.
    
    The remaining increments are pure cascade — a dependency bump and
    nothing else.
    
    ```
    bytesbuf 0.8.0 ─┬─> bytesbuf_io 0.8.0
                    ├─> multitude 0.8.0
                    ├─> cachet 0.11.0
                    ├─> http_extensions 0.9.0 ─┬─> fetch_hyper 0.6.0 ─┐
                    ├─> fetch_azure 0.5.0      └─> seatbelt_http 0.7.0┤
                    └─> fetch 0.15.0 <─────────────────────────────────┘
    
    thread_aware 0.9.0 ─┬─> bytesbuf 0.8.0
                        ├─> anyspawn 0.7.0 ──> anyspawn_azure 0.2.0
                        ├─> cachet_memory 0.6.0
                        ├─> seatbelt 0.7.0
                        ├─> uniflight 0.4.0
                        └─> tick 0.5.0
    
    ohno 0.4.0 ─┬─> ohno_macros 0.4.0 (dependency; bumped first)
                ├─> fetch_tls 0.3.0 ──> fetch 0.15.0, fetch_hyper 0.6.0
                ├─> templated_uri_macros_impl 0.4.0 ──> templated_uri_macros 0.4.0 ─┐
                ├─> bytesbuf_io, cachet_memory, cachet_tier, fetch, fetch_hyper     │
                ├─> http_extensions, recoverable, seatbelt, seatbelt_http, tick     │
                └─> templated_uri 0.4.0 <───────────────────────────────────────────┘
    
    templated_uri 0.4.0 ──> fetch, fetch_hyper, http_extensions, seatbelt_http
    
    recoverable 0.2.0 ─┬─> seatbelt 0.7.0
                       ├─> http_extensions 0.9.0
                       └─> cachet_tier 0.3.0 ─┬─> cachet_memory 0.6.0
                                              ├─> cachet_service 0.3.0 ──> cachet 0.11.0
                                              └─> cachet 0.11.0
    
    plurality 0.2.1 ──> bytesbuf 0.8.0        (compatible; no cascade)
    ```
    
    ## Minimum supported Rust version
    
    The workspace floor moved to 1.93.1 in #629, pulled in by `serial_test`
    4.0.1. Every released changelog now records it. The requirement is
    inherited from `[workspace.package]`, so it applies to all crates here
    including the patch-level releases — a consumer on 1.93.0 cannot resolve
    them. No consumer-visible dependency needs 1.93.1; the floor comes from
    a test-only crate.
    
    ## Publishing order
    
    `bytesbuf` must be published before `multitude`. `multitude`'s
    `bytesbuf` feature enables `bytesbuf/std`, which does not exist in the
    published `bytesbuf` 0.7.0, so `multitude` is unpublishable until
    `bytesbuf` 0.8.0 is on crates.io. Likewise `ohno_macros` precedes
    `ohno`, and `templated_uri_macros_impl` precedes `templated_uri_macros`.
    
    ## Notes for the reviewer
    
    `crates/plurality/CHANGELOG.md` carried an `## [Unreleased]` heading
    whose contents had in fact shipped. It is retitled to `## [0.2.0] -
    2026-07-24`, matching the `plurality-v0.2.0` tag, and the new 0.2.1
    section sits above it.
    
    `recoverable` 0.1.7, `ohno_macros` 0.3.5 and the crates listed below are
    published on crates.io but have no Git tag. Release tooling that derives
    its changelog range from tags will pick up a stale baseline for them;
    both needed their baseline established from the version-bump commit
    instead. Worth reconciling separately.
    
    `Cargo.lock` legitimately contains a second `ohno` 0.3.5 entry alongside
    the workspace's 0.4.0. It is pulled from crates.io by the third-party
    `cpulist` crate and predates this change; no workspace member resolves
    to it.
    
    `thread_aware_macros` 0.7.5, `thread_aware_macros_impl` 0.7.4,
    `data_privacy_core` 0.1.2, `data_privacy_macros` 0.10.3,
    `data_privacy_macros_impl` 0.10.3 and `fetch_options` 0.2.3 are left
    untouched.
    
