    fix(ohno)!: improve `#[display]` and `#[error]` diagnostics (#634)
    
    ## What this changes
    
    The macro reported several of its own mistakes through the code it
    generates. Users saw errors about fields they never declared, spanned at
    a derive rather than at anything they wrote. This PR moves that
    reporting into the macro, adds the validation it needs, and pins the
    spans with a trybuild UI suite.
    
    Implicit `self` scoping stays the syntax model for `#[display(...)]`
    arguments. The new validation rejects some inputs that the macro
    previously accepted and expanded into wrong or unbuildable code.
    
    ## Details
    
    - `#[display(...)]` arguments are validated before expansion. The macro
    walks each argument to its leftmost term and checks that term names a
    field. A misspelled root, a `self.` prefix, or a root that cannot follow
    a dot is reported at the term itself.
    - The `OhnoCore` field that `#[ohno::error]` injects is marked with a
    reserved doc string, not with `#[error]`. `#[error]` therefore takes no
    arguments at all, and the injected field is excluded from every
    "available fields" list. The marker carries a nonce, because a doc
    string can only fail to match and a marker that read as prose would let
    an ordinary doc comment hide the field it documents.
    - A core field the user declares is data. It is passed to the generated
    constructors, appears in `Debug`, and can be interpolated. It is never
    read for `source()`, the backtrace, or enrichment.
    - `#[ohno::error]` rejects a field marked `#[error]`. The attribute
    always generates the error representation from the field it injects. The
    diagnostic names both remedies.
    - A second `#[error]` marker is reported instead of being resolved by
    declaration order.
    - Tuple indices resolve. `{0}` works, and the index check shares its
    source of truth with the available-fields list.
    - Raw identifiers keep their prefix. A field named `r#type` is rebuilt
    with `Ident::new_raw` instead of panicking, and is echoed back as
    `r#type`.
    - Multi-token diagnostics use a new `bail_spanned!` helper built on
    `Error::new_spanned`. A node span collapses to its first token where
    `Span::join` is absent, so one snapshot cannot match both toolchains.
    - Crate docs and README gain a "Format Arguments" section with an
    accepted/rejected table.
    
    ## Effects
    
    - A positional tuple index that addressed the injected core is now
    rejected. On an error with one declared tuple field, `#[display("bad:
    {}", 1)]` expanded to `&self.1`, compiled, and formatted the error's own
    chain. It now fails as an unknown field.
    - `#[error(generated)]` is rejected. That spelling was the macro's own
    handoff and is no longer part of the attribute's grammar.
    - `#[ohno::error]` combined with an `#[error]` marker is now a hard
    error.
    - An explicit `#[error]` marker still designates a core reached through
    an alias or a rename. Auto-detection still matches the final path
    segment only.
    - The `thiserror` spelling `self.path.display()` stays an error.
    Migration still needs an edit; the diagnostic now explains why.
    - `display.rs` and `field_detection.rs` grow by about 500 lines.
    `convert_expr_to_field_access` becomes fallible, so there are more error
    paths to keep correct.
