    feat(tick): add EcmaScript timestamp format (#612)
    
    [Copilot speaking]
    
    ## Summary
    
    Adds `EcmaScript` as a first-class `tick::fmt` timestamp format,
    alongside `Iso8601`, `Rfc2822`, and `UnixSeconds`. It formats the
    ECMAScript Date Time String Format produced by
    `Date.prototype.toISOString`, with exactly three fractional digits and
    the UTC designator `Z`.
    
    For years `0000` through `9999`, output has the fixed 24-character shape
    `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.sssZ`. On platforms whose `SystemTime` supports
    earlier years, negative years use the ECMAScript expanded-year form,
    such as `-009999-01-02T01:59:59.000Z`.
    
    ## API and behavior
    
    - Adds `tick::fmt::EcmaScript` with `MAX`, `UNIX_EPOCH`, `FromStr`,
    `Display`, `TryFrom<SystemTime>`, infallible conversion to `SystemTime`,
    serde support, and thread-awareness conformance.
    - Parses RFC 3339 timestamps and the expanded-year syntax supported by
    `jiff::Timestamp`, normalizing UTC offsets to `Z`.
    - Canonicalizes values to millisecond precision at construction. Finer
    precision is floored rather than rounded, including before the Unix
    epoch, so equality, ordering, hashing, display, and serialization agree.
    - Validates constructed values against the platform's `SystemTime`
    range. Any `EcmaScript` value can therefore be converted to `SystemTime`
    without a range panic.
    - Does not provide direct conversions between timestamp format types.
    Conversions use `SystemTime` as the explicit fallible bridge, consistent
    with the rest of `tick::fmt`.
    - Adds `SystemTimeExt::display_ecmascript()`, an infallible formatter
    that saturates values outside jiff's supported timestamp range.
    - Extends the `tick::fmt` module documentation, examples, serde
    coverage, range tests, and shared format tests for `EcmaScript`.
    
    ## Unix seconds consistency
    
    `UnixSeconds` now stores only canonical whole-second values.
    Construction floors sub-second `Duration` values to whole seconds,
    keeping equality, ordering, hashing, display, and serialization
    consistent. `UnixSeconds::MAX` is the whole-second floor of the common
    upper timestamp boundary: `253402207200` (`9999-12-30T22:00:00Z`).
    
    Pre-epoch `SystemTime` values remain rejected because `UnixSeconds`
    represents an unsigned offset from the Unix epoch.
    
    ## Validation
    
    - Windows: 187 `tick` unit tests and 61 doctests pass.
    - Linux/WSL: all ECMAScript-specific tests, including expanded negative
    years, and the shared platform-range test pass.
    - `clippy`, `format-check`, `spellcheck`, and README generation pass.
    
