    fix(tick)!: validate fmt values at construction, drop cross-format conversions (#614)
    
    Fixes three out-of-range conversion defects in the `tick::fmt` family.
    Each produced a
    wrong value or a panic for instants outside a format's representable
    range.
    
    | Work item | Defect |
    | --- | --- |
    | AB#7661495 | `to_unix_epoch_duration` applied `unsigned_abs()` to a
    signed epoch offset, so pre-epoch instants silently **mirrored** to the
    same distance *after* the epoch (`1969-12-31T23:59:59Z` became
    `UnixSeconds(1)`). |
    | AB#7661499 | `Display for Rfc2822` returned `fmt::Error` for years
    outside `0000..=9999`, which `ToString`/`format!`/serde turn into a
    **panic**. Reachable through the entirely safe, infallible
    `From<Iso8601>` conversion. |
    | AB#7663342 | `From<X> for SystemTime` delegated to jiff, whose
    `From<Timestamp> for SystemTime` wraps `checked_add`/`checked_sub` in
    `.expect()`. Where `SystemTime` is FILETIME based (epoch
    `1601-01-01T00:00:00Z`), every earlier instant is unrepresentable and
    the conversion **panics**. |
    
    ## BREAKING CHANGE
    
    The six cross-format conversions are removed:
    
    ```rust
    impl From<Rfc2822>     for Iso8601      // removed
    impl From<UnixSeconds> for Iso8601      // removed
    impl From<Iso8601>     for Rfc2822      // removed
    impl From<UnixSeconds> for Rfc2822      // removed
    impl From<Iso8601>     for UnixSeconds  // removed
    impl From<Rfc2822>     for UnixSeconds  // removed
    ```
    
    Each format has a different representable range, so these conversions
    were necessarily
    lossy or fallible while presenting as infallible -- which is exactly
    what produced
    AB#7661495 and made AB#7661499 reachable. Convert through `SystemTime`
    instead, where the
    fallible step is explicit:
    
    ```rust
    // before
    let rfc = Rfc2822::from(iso);
    
    // after
    let rfc = Rfc2822::try_from(SystemTime::from(iso))?;
    ```
    
    Two further behaviour changes:
    
    - `TryFrom<SystemTime> for UnixSeconds` now **rejects** pre-epoch
    instants instead of
      silently clamping them to the epoch.
    - `FromStr` for `Iso8601` and `Rfc2822` now **rejects** instants the
    platform's `SystemTime`
    cannot represent, rather than accepting a value that panics on
    conversion.
    
    No public item is added. `anvil-semver-check` is advisory for changes
    between unreleased
    commits, so it reports rather than blocks.
    
    ## Approach
    
    All three defects share one root cause: a value could be constructed
    that the target could
    not represent, and the conversion then had to invent something. So the
    fix is to make that
    value unconstructable.
    
    **Every format validates on construction.** Parsing, and converting from
    a `SystemTime`,
    reject any instant the platform's `SystemTime` cannot represent.
    `Rfc2822` additionally
    rejects years before 0, which RFC 2822 has no four-digit form for.
    Because the invariant
    holds by construction:
    
    - `From<X> for SystemTime` is genuinely infallible -- nothing to
    saturate to, and no need to
      discover the platform's bound at runtime.
    - `Rfc2822` cannot hold a year its `Display` is unable to encode, so
    `to_string` cannot panic.
    - Pre-epoch instants are rejected rather than clamped, so nothing is
    mirrored.
    
    ### Changes
    
    - **`fmt/mod.rs`** -- `checked_system_time` asks the platform whether a
    single instant is
    representable; `ensure_system_time_representable` is applied at every
    construction point;
    `to_system_time` performs the now-guaranteed conversion for `Rfc2822`,
    whose tests build
    out-of-range values directly and so benefit from a documented
    `.expect()` message.
    `Iso8601` converts through jiff directly and `UnixSeconds` through its
    own checked add,
      each documenting the invariant in place.
    - **`Rfc2822`** -- a private `MIN_TIMESTAMP` (`Sat, 01 Jan 0000 00:00:00
    GMT`) plus a private
      `new_checked` that every construction path routes through.
    - **`Iso8601`, `UnixSeconds`** -- validated on construction;
    `UnixSeconds` rejects pre-epoch
      `SystemTime` values.
    - **Docs** -- the module documents the reject-on-construction policy and
    why cross-format
    conversion is deliberately unsupported. The `MAX` doc comments, which
    claimed
    `31 December 9999 23:59:59 UTC` rather than the instant actually stored,
    are corrected and
      guarded by a test.
    
    `ecma_script.rs` is referenced by all three work items but does not
    exist on `main` yet, so
    it is out of scope. It will inherit the validated construction pattern
    when it lands.
    
    ## AB#7663342 is platform-dependent -- and real
    
    The panic does not reproduce on every Windows toolchain. It depends on
    how `std` implements
    `SystemTime`:
    
    | Toolchain | Earliest representable `SystemTime` |
    | --- | --- |
    | `ms-prod` (1.93.1) | epoch minus ~922,337,203,685 s (covers the whole
    range) |
    | `ms-prod-1.95`, `ms-nightly`, CI stable 1.97 | epoch minus exactly
    11,644,473,600 s = `1601-01-01T00:00:00Z` |
    
    On the FILETIME-based toolchains the original code panics exactly as
    reported:
    
    ```
    1601-01-01T00:00:00Z: old = ok,    new = ok
    1000-01-01T00:00:00Z: old = PANIC, new = rejected at parse
    0001-01-01T00:00:00Z: old = PANIC, new = rejected at parse
    ```
    
    Every test is written against the platform's own arithmetic rather than
    an assumed range, so
    the suite is meaningful on both kinds of platform, and it is run against
    both locally.
    
    ## Validation
    
    - `cargo test -p tick --all-features --locked` on **both** a wide-range
    toolchain
    (`ms-prod` 1.93.1) and a FILETIME-limited one (`ms-prod-1.95`) -- green
    on both
    - `cargo test -p http_extensions --all-features --locked` -- green
    (consumes `Rfc2822`)
    - `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features --locked` -- clean
    - `cargo +ms-prod-1.93 check -p tick --all-features --all-targets` --
    MSRV clean
    - `cargo fmt --check` with `unstable-rustfmt.toml` -- clean
    - `cargo llvm-cov` -- 100% line coverage for `tick`
    - 163 unit tests + 59 doctests
    
    ## Review
    
    Reviewed by three models (Opus, Sonnet, GPT) plus human review. Opus and
    GPT found no issues.
    Sonnet caught an incorrect `Iso8601::MAX` rendering in the docs. Copilot
    review flagged four
    further `MAX` doc inconsistencies, and four claims that the test modules
    would not compile --
    those were incorrect, since `use super::*` brings `SystemTime` into
    scope, verified by a
    clean `--all-targets` build on two toolchains.
    
    @martintmk directed the design above -- dropping cross-format
    conversions and validating at
    parse time -- which replaced an earlier saturating approach that probed
    for the platform
    bound at runtime.
    
    Three further review rounds refined it:
    
    - `From<Iso8601>` and `From<Rfc2822> for SystemTime` both convert
    through jiff directly; the
      `to_system_time` indirection is gone.
    - `Rfc2822::MIN_TIMESTAMP` is *not* redundant with
    `ensure_system_time_representable`: the two
    guard different bounds and coincide only on FILETIME-based platforms,
    whose floor already
    sits above year 0. It is now parsed from `"Sat, 01 Jan 0000 00:00:00
    GMT"` rather than
      written as an epoch offset.
    - The three tuple fields are private rather than `pub(super)`, so the
    checked-construction
      invariant is structural instead of a convention.
    - Documentation corrections: `Iso8601` output is RFC 3339 only for years
    `0000` through
    `9999`; `Clock::system_time_as` panics when the target format cannot
    represent the clock's
    instant; `SystemTime` bounds resolution as well as range, so a round
    trip truncates to the
    platform's grid; and the `Rfc2822` range example no longer claims to
    exercise a check it
      never reaches.
    
    Two suggestions were declined with reasons on the threads: a public
    `Rfc2822::MIN` would be a
    constant whose `SystemTime` conversion panics on FILETIME platforms,
    violating the very
    invariant this PR establishes, and a hand-written CHANGELOG entry is
    left to the release
    tooling that generates changelogs from Git history.
    
