    fix(bytesbuf): scale Linux sysinfo memory by mem_unit (#594)
    
    [Copilot speaking]
    
    ## Summary
    
    `system_memory()` on Linux read `sysinfo.totalram` directly as a byte
    count. On Linux, `sysinfo` memory amounts are expressed in units of
    `mem_unit` bytes, so ignoring `mem_unit` under-reports total RAM on
    kernels that use a larger unit. That value gates whether the
    memory-hungry giant-allocation tests run (`system_memory() <
    10_000_000_000` skips them), so under-reporting caused those tests to be
    skipped spuriously.
    
    This scales `totalram` by `mem_unit`, treating a reported unit of `0` as
    `1` (legacy behavior from before the field existed) and using
    `checked_mul` to guard against overflow.
    
    ## Validation
    
    - `cargo check` and `cargo clippy --lib` clean for
    `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` (exercises the changed Linux-gated path).
    - `cargo check` clean on the default Windows target.
    - `just format` and `just spellcheck` clean.
    
