    perf(bytesbuf): fuse single-span read/write fast paths + bootstrap benchmarking agent docs (#518)
    
    ## Summary
    
    Adds fused single-span fast paths to the hot read and write operations
    of `bytesbuf`, plus the Criterion and Callgrind benchmark coverage that
    validates and guards them. Every optimization is backed by both a
    Callgrind instruction-count (`Ir`) measurement and a properly scoped
    wall-clock benchmark (inputs built outside the timed region), and both
    metrics agree on the direction.
    
    This branch also adds the benchmarking-guidelines documentation
    (`docs/benchmarks.md`, `docs/callgrind-benchmarks.md`, `docs/naming.md`,
    `docs/performance.md`) and the related AGENTS notes that the new
    benchmarks follow (based on ox-sdk and folo reference implementations).
    
    ## Optimizations
    
    - **`BytesView::range` single-span fast path** — when the requested
    range lies entirely within the first span (always true for a
    single-block sequence, and common when peeking a header), return a
    sub-slice of that one span with no cross-span accounting. `Ir` -30%,
    wall 36.6 → 27.1 ns (-26%).
    - **`GlobalPool` single-block reserve** — the common reservation is one
    block; build the buffer straight from it via `BytesBuf::from_block`,
    skipping the iterator/collect/sum machinery of the multi-block path.
    `Ir` -18..-28%, wall (copied_tiny) 134 → 117 ns (-13%), (reserve_more)
    150 → 130 ns (-14%).
    - **`BytesView::get_byte` fused read** — read the front byte and advance
    within the same span in a single span lookup. `Ir` -57%, wall 2.99 →
    1.77 ns/byte (-41%).
    - **`get_num_le`/`be`/`ne` first-span read** — read the value directly
    from the first span when it does not straddle a boundary
    (`get_num_in_first_span`), falling back to buffered assembly otherwise.
    `Ir` -46%, wall 3.20 → 2.29 ns/u32 (-28%).
    - **`put_num_le`/`be`/`ne` fused write** — route through `put_small` so
    a numeric write that fits in the first unfilled slice is a single copy.
    `Ir` -27%, wall 4.61 → 4.18 ns/write (-9%).
    - **`put_slice` fused write** — same `put_small` fast path for small
    slices; multi-slice writes use the looped path. `Ir` -28%, wall 5.15 →
    4.48 ns/write (-13%).
    
    Each fast path is behavior-preserving: when its precondition does not
    hold (range crosses a span boundary, multi-block reserve, value
    straddles a boundary, slice spans multiple slices), the call routes to
    the existing buffered path and produces an identical result.
    
    ## Benchmarks
    
    - Criterion: `get_byte_drain`, `get_num_le_drain` (BytesView) and
    `put_slice`, `put_num_le` (BytesBuf), all using setup-outside-timing
    batching.
    - Callgrind: `view_cg` and `buf_cg`, pairing one-to-one with the
    Criterion benches above and isolating a single call per scenario so
    drops fall outside the measured region.
    
    ## Maintainability
    
    `get_num_in_first_span` is a behavior-preserving fast path: returning
    `None` (or declining the value that exactly fills the first span) routes
    the caller to the buffered path, which yields an identical result.
    Mutations that disable it are functionally equivalent and uncatchable by
    behavioral tests, so it is marked `#[cfg_attr(test, mutants::skip)]`
    with rationale. Scoped mutation testing of the remaining new fast-path
    code is clean (all mutants caught).
    
    `MAX_INLINE_SPANS` carries an inline tuning note: benchmark and gather a
    real-world distribution of span counts before re-tuning it, since
    lowering it helps small sequences but regresses 5–8 span sequences and
    raising it is slower across the board.
    
    ## Tooling
    
    `GUNGRAUN_RUNNER_VERSION` in `constants.env` is aligned with the locked
    `gungraun` library version (0.19.2) so the Callgrind runner matches the
    library and `bench-cg` runs; the install line in
    `docs/callgrind-benchmarks.md` matches.
    
    ## Validation
    
    - `cargo test -p bytesbuf --all-features`: 182 unit + 61 doctests pass.
    - `cargo clippy -p bytesbuf --all-targets --all-features` with `-D
    warnings`: clean.
    - `just format`, `just readme`, `just spellcheck`: clean.
    - Callgrind benches compiled and ran under Valgrind (WSL), producing
    deterministic instruction counts for all scenarios.
    - Scoped mutation testing of the new fast-path functions: all mutants
    caught.
    
