    fix(cachet): de-flake every_helper_emits_its_event test (#500)
    
    ## Summary
    
    Fixes the flaky
    `cachet::telemetry::cache::tests::every_helper_emits_its_event` reported
    in ADO #7485979.
    
    ## Root cause
    
    A *different* test in the cachet unit-test binary --
    `logging_enabled_without_subscriber_is_noop` -- exercises cachet's
    `tracing::error!` / `tracing::info!` paths with no `tracing` subscriber
    installed on the current thread. When it wins the race to be the first
    thread to register one of those callsites, it poisons the global
    `tracing-core` callsite interest cache, suppressing those events on
    every other thread for the rest of the binary's lifetime.
    
    The mechanism:
    
    1. `tracing-core::callsite::DefaultCallsite::register` -- called the
    first time a `tracing::*!` macro is hit -- invokes
    `rebuild_callsite_interest(self, &DISPATCHERS.rebuilder())`.
    2. `Dispatchers::has_just_one` is `true` whenever the registered
    dispatcher count is `<= 1`, including 0.
    3. In that case the `Rebuilder::JustOne` fast path calls
    `dispatcher::get_default()` **on the registering thread** instead of
    iterating registered dispatchers.
    4. With no thread-local default and no global default, `get_default()`
    resolves to `NoSubscriber`, whose `register_callsite` returns
    `Interest::never()`.
    5. That `Interest::never()` is cached process-wide. Every subsequent
    emission at that callsite -- from ANY thread, regardless of which
    subscriber that thread later installs -- is silently suppressed.
    
    The flake surface in `every_helper_emits_its_event` was precisely the
    two rarest callsites in the binary (`cache.get_error` and
    `cache.insert_rejected`), because the no-subscriber test wins the
    registration race for them most often. The common callsites (`cache.hit`
    etc.) get registered first by tests that DO have subscribers, so they
    cache as enabled.
    
    ## Fix
    
    Move the no-subscriber test to its own integration-test binary at
    `crates/cachet/tests/no_subscriber.rs`. Each integration test compiles
    to a separate executable with its own `tracing-core` callsite registry,
    so the scenario cannot poison any other test binary's cache. The test's
    intent is fully preserved -- it really does run cachet's tracing
    emission paths with no subscriber installed.
    
    The new test drives the same scenarios through the public `Cache` API:
    
    - Failing `MockCache` storage exercises the error events
    (`cache.get_error`, `cache.insert_error`, `cache.invalidate_error`,
    `cache.clear_error`).
    - A working `MockCache` with `InsertPolicy::never()` exercises
    `cache.miss` / `cache.insert_rejected`.
    - A working `MockCache` with the default policy exercises `cache.hit` /
    `cache.inserted` and the success path of `complete_operation`.
    
    ## Rejected alternatives
    
    - **Install a discarding subscriber inside the unit test** (my earlier
    commit e2053013): rejected -- this defeats the test's intent. The test
    exists to verify cachet's emission paths tolerate the absence of a
    subscriber.
    - **Invalidate the callsite interest cache after the test**: the
    corruption affects tests running CONCURRENTLY with the no-subscriber
    test, not just those running after it, so healing afterward is too late.
    - **Serialize the test with all potential victims** (`serial_test` crate
    or a shared mutex): would require annotating many other tests in the
    binary and adds a new dependency for what is fundamentally an isolation
    problem.
    - **Refactor `every_helper_emits_its_event` to share one subscriber**
    (my earliest commit b1365593): rejected -- stress testing showed it
    didn't actually fix anything (~1.4% post-fix vs ~1.3% pre-fix).
    
    ## Verification
    
    Stress test (Windows, `--test-threads=32`):
    
    | Scenario | Pre-fix | Post-fix |
    |---|---|---|
    | Full unit binary | 4/300 (~1.3%) | **0/300** |
    | `telemetry::cache::tests::` filter | 25/100 (25%) | **0/200** |
    
    The new integration test passes consistently and runs in complete
    isolation.
    
