    feat(bytesbuf): GlobalPool uses smaller memory blocks for smaller reservation requests (#254)
    
    feat(bytesbuf): GlobalPool uses smaller memory blocks for smaller
    reservation requests
    
    Previously, we used 64KB blocks, always. This was just a placeholder
    implementation, really, so now we take a first step forward by adding
    1KB, 4KB and 16KB block sizes. We use whatever is the smallest block
    that fits for the requested memory reservation (anything above 64KB gets
    multiple 64KB blocks).
    
    This can help avoid wasted memory if we have a lot of small allocations.
    Not specifically the case with our I/O benchmarks so far but since
    bytesbuf is more generic than just I/O, this can become of some
    relevance when we start talking about data processing (e.g.
    serialization of URL templates).
    
    Also added some basic metrics to GlobalPool to allow us to understand
    memory reservation efficiency and drive future design decisions in this
    domain with a larger degree of data-basis.
    
    On the flip side, this additional logic in reserve() does slow down
    memory reservation somewhat (20% for large reservations). I have not
    seen our memory pool show up on profiler traces so far, so hopefully
    this change is insignificant enough to have a real world impact but
    let's keep it in mind in case we notice something.

